WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.476 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.476 --> 00:00:02.952 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:02.952 --> 00:00:04.920 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:11.055 align:middle line:90%


00:00:11.055 --> 00:00:11.930 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: All right.

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:16.968 align:middle line:84%
Well, today's guest lecturer is
James Shepherd with Quantile--

00:00:16.968 --> 00:00:18.260 align:middle line:90%
JAMES SHEPHERD: Quantile, yeah.

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:23.240 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: --which is a wholly
owned subsidiary of LSEG.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:31.010 align:middle line:84%
And your background in
quant finance is quite long.

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:33.818 align:middle line:84%
And so I'll let you share
some of the highlights.

00:00:33.818 --> 00:00:34.610 align:middle line:90%
JAMES SHEPHERD: OK.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:35.640 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Thank you
very much, James.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:36.120 align:middle line:84%
JAMES SHEPHERD:
Thank you very much.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:36.750 align:middle line:90%
Do I need that.

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:37.010 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: No.

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:37.802 align:middle line:90%
JAMES SHEPHERD: OK.

00:00:37.802 --> 00:00:38.670 align:middle line:90%
Thank you very much.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.692 align:middle line:90%
Thank you for having me.

00:00:40.692 --> 00:00:42.900 align:middle line:84%
This supposed to finish
about-- so a quarter to 4:00.

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.180 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:45.970 align:middle line:84%
I'd like to leave some
time for questions.

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:46.970 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Until-- yeah.

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:47.900 align:middle line:90%
10 to 4:00.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:48.980 align:middle line:90%
JAMES SHEPHERD: OK.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:50.490 align:middle line:84%
Thank you very
much for having me.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:51.570 align:middle line:90%
Yes, I'm James.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:54.650 align:middle line:84%
Yes, I work for
Quantile Technologies.

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:59.730 align:middle line:84%
That's a fintech company,
founded in about 2016,

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.980 align:middle line:84%
to do counterparty
risk optimization,

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:05.700 align:middle line:84%
which is the subject
of today's talk.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.380 align:middle line:84%
A couple of years
ago, we got bought out

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:09.690 align:middle line:84%
by the London Stock
Exchange Group, which

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:12.940 align:middle line:90%
is why my email now says LSEG.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:16.840 align:middle line:84%
Prior to that, I was at Morgan
Stanley for quite a long time,

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.480 align:middle line:84%
which is where I know
Vasiliy and Jake from,

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:21.125 align:middle line:90%
which is how I've ended up here.

00:01:21.125 --> 00:01:23.502 align:middle line:90%


00:01:23.502 --> 00:01:24.960 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
talk today, we're

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:29.760 align:middle line:84%
going to work our way up
to talking about optimizing

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:31.710 align:middle line:84%
a thing called initial
margin, which is

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:35.220 align:middle line:90%
a measure of counterparty risk.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:37.410 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
start off talking

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:39.010 align:middle line:90%
about different types of risk.

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:39.600 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.880 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:42.900 align:middle line:90%


00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.070 align:middle line:84%
JAMES SHEPHERD: I've just
emailed them to Peter.

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:46.030 align:middle line:90%
Sorry about that.

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:47.742 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:47.742 --> 00:01:48.037 align:middle line:90%


00:01:48.037 --> 00:01:49.620 align:middle line:84%
JAMES SHEPHERD: The
first view is just

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:53.070 align:middle line:84%
kind of like warmup stuff, so
you're not missing too much.

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:56.640 align:middle line:84%
So the first part of the talk,
we're going to talk about

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.190 align:middle line:84%
are different types
of risk that you

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:00.620 align:middle line:90%
have with derivative trading.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.620 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to talk
about expected shortfall

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:05.515 align:middle line:90%
and value at risk.

00:02:05.515 --> 00:02:06.390 align:middle line:90%
Just out of interest?

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:08.690 align:middle line:84%
Do people know what
these terms are,

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:12.406 align:middle line:84%
or is it worth my explaining
them a little bit?

00:02:12.406 --> 00:02:13.400 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Explain.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:16.310 align:middle line:84%
JAMES SHEPHERD:
OK, I will do that.

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:19.550 align:middle line:84%
Then we'll talk about how
that relates to initial margin

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:20.970 align:middle line:90%
and counterparty risk.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:24.590 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll work
our way up to how

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:29.240 align:middle line:84%
we optimize this initial
margin within a network

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:32.700 align:middle line:90%
of financial institutions.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:35.480 align:middle line:84%
And we'll talk a lot
about the challenges

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:38.720 align:middle line:90%
that you get in the real world.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.810 align:middle line:84%
Because this is a maths
talk, I feel morally obliged

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:44.940 align:middle line:84%
to chuck some equations in
the thing from time to time.

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:47.630 align:middle line:84%
But largely, I'll skip over
that and talk a little bit

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:50.870 align:middle line:84%
about some of the intuition,
some of the toy models.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:52.620 align:middle line:84%
And I've put a
bunch of references.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.245 align:middle line:84%
When you get the
thing, there's a bunch

00:02:54.245 --> 00:02:57.370 align:middle line:84%
of references at the end, which
has got some of the proper maths

00:02:57.370 --> 00:02:59.530 align:middle line:84%
and proofs and
more detailed stuff

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:04.190 align:middle line:84%
behind it, if you're
interested in that.

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:06.420 align:middle line:84%
So in the shape of
derivatives, when

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.170 align:middle line:84%
we talk about the
different kinds of risks

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:11.380 align:middle line:84%
that can arise when you're
trading derivatives,

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:13.760 align:middle line:84%
market risk is probably
the most common one.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.240 align:middle line:84%
So that's the value of the
derivative goes up and down

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:19.570 align:middle line:90%
as the market changes.

00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:25.310 align:middle line:84%
Credit risk, again, is
reasonably well understood.

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:33.710 align:middle line:84%
It's the rise when you've got
exposure to a debtor defaulting.

00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:37.750 align:middle line:84%
Operational risk is largely
when somebody screws up.

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:39.790 align:middle line:84%
It's probably actually
the hardest one

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:42.440 align:middle line:84%
to do any mathematical
modeling on operational risk

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:47.380 align:middle line:84%
because it's quite hard to model
people just messing up, which

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:49.690 align:middle line:90%
is really what that is.

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:53.980 align:middle line:84%
Liquidity risk is when you've
got a transaction, which

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:58.388 align:middle line:84%
is well, as the name
suggests, illiquid,

00:03:58.388 --> 00:04:00.680 align:middle line:84%
which can happen when you've
got a very large position.

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:02.770 align:middle line:84%
So if you're trying to
sell a massive position

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:05.337 align:middle line:84%
in some particular stock,
you start selling that thing.

00:04:05.337 --> 00:04:06.920 align:middle line:84%
And then because you
start selling it,

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:08.000 align:middle line:90%
that moves the market.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.390 align:middle line:84%
And so you can't sell the
whole thing at the position

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:13.000 align:middle line:84%
that the market was
when you started.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:17.660 align:middle line:84%
So liquidity risk happens it's
a kind of obscure instrument.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:19.459 align:middle line:84%
And we'll come
back to it in this.

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:23.960 align:middle line:84%
It happens again if you've got
a very, very large position.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:26.230 align:middle line:84%
And what we're mostly
going to talk about today

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:30.640 align:middle line:84%
is counterparty risk, which
is the exposure that you've

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:36.910 align:middle line:84%
got when you trade derivatives
to a counterparty defaulting.

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:40.570 align:middle line:84%
I think maybe a question
you might have here

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:43.450 align:middle line:84%
is, what's the difference
between counterparty risk

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:45.790 align:middle line:90%
and credit risk?

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:47.950 align:middle line:84%
So the example you can
have to differentiate

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:51.130 align:middle line:84%
this is, if I do a trade
with Goldman Sachs,

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:53.380 align:middle line:84%
let's say, a credit
derivative swap that

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:57.800 align:middle line:84%
has some JPMorgan corporate
bond as the underlying,

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:00.830 align:middle line:84%
then I have credit risk
with respect to JPMorgan.

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:04.190 align:middle line:84%
Because if JPMorgan defaults
on its corporate bond,

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:07.610 align:middle line:84%
then the value of my
derivative will change.

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:10.600 align:middle line:84%
And I have counterparty
risk to Goldman Sachs

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:12.880 align:middle line:84%
because, depending
on what happens

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:15.670 align:middle line:84%
to the value of that derivative,
Goldman Sachs may or may not

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:18.860 align:middle line:90%
pay me that amount of money.

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:22.780 align:middle line:84%
That's the difference
between those two.

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:23.445 align:middle line:90%
Make sense?

00:05:23.445 --> 00:05:26.575 align:middle line:90%


00:05:26.575 --> 00:05:28.940 align:middle line:84%
So once you've got
a type of risk,

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:31.550 align:middle line:84%
once you've got some
risk, generally speaking,

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:34.240 align:middle line:84%
you want to mitigate
it, i.e., reduce either

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:36.040 align:middle line:84%
the probability of it
happening, or reduce

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:38.560 align:middle line:84%
the impact of that
risk happening, not

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:42.700 align:middle line:84%
just for counterparty risk,
for all kinds of risk.

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:45.640 align:middle line:84%
Hopefully, reasonably obvious
that people want to try and make

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:49.580 align:middle line:84%
the impact of once you've
identified the risk,

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:51.140 align:middle line:90%
make the impact of that smaller.

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:54.760 align:middle line:84%
So there's a bunch of different
ways that you can do that.

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:58.460 align:middle line:84%
We're largely going day--
to talk about the hedging,

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:00.662 align:middle line:84%
which means book some
trades to offset--

00:06:00.662 --> 00:06:03.370 align:middle line:84%
book some new trades, which will
offset the risk that you've just

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:05.230 align:middle line:90%
discovered that you've got.

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:08.650 align:middle line:84%
And we'll talk about
collateralization,

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:13.420 align:middle line:84%
which is really paying margin
payments from the person you've

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:15.520 align:middle line:84%
got-- the person
you're exposed to,

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:17.140 align:middle line:84%
you would collect
a margin payment

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:19.510 align:middle line:84%
from them, which is kind of
like an insurance payment

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:20.960 align:middle line:90%
against them defaulting.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:24.190 align:middle line:84%
And that's mostly what we're
going to talk about today.

00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:27.130 align:middle line:84%
There are other
things you can do.

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:30.118 align:middle line:84%
And there's a thing called
central clearing counterparties,

00:06:30.118 --> 00:06:31.910 align:middle line:84%
which I thought nobody
would have heard of.

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:34.870 align:middle line:84%
But apparently, someone's--
Andrew Gunstensen has already

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:36.230 align:middle line:90%
mentioned this a little bit.

00:06:36.230 --> 00:06:38.950 align:middle line:84%
So I hope I might
skip over this slide

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:44.770 align:middle line:84%
because they are important in
the trading of derivatives.

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:48.300 align:middle line:90%
So I have a quick slide on CCPs.

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:50.970 align:middle line:84%
So normally, you
trade derivatives,

00:06:50.970 --> 00:06:52.330 align:middle line:90%
and they're bilateral trades.

00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:54.700 align:middle line:84%
So JPMorgan trades with
Goldman Sachs and so on.

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:57.450 align:middle line:84%
So we've got here,
you've got four parties.

00:06:57.450 --> 00:07:00.150 align:middle line:84%
Say, A trades with
B. A trades with C.

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:03.360 align:middle line:84%
It's got a position
of 100 versus B. 125

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:05.320 align:middle line:84%
in the opposite
direction versus C.

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:09.130 align:middle line:84%
And what a central
clearing counterparty does,

00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:11.400 align:middle line:84%
as the name kind of
suggests, is that it

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:14.580 align:middle line:84%
steps into the middle
of all these trades.

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:18.280 align:middle line:84%
And so instead of A
facing B and A facing C,

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:20.380 align:middle line:90%
A just faces the CCP.

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:24.030 align:middle line:84%
And so does B, and so
does C, and so does D.

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:26.410 align:middle line:84%
Just by doing that you
haven't really achieved much.

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:28.260 align:middle line:84%
You've just changed
your counterparty risk

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:35.190 align:middle line:84%
from being versus B and
C to versus the CCP.

00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:38.730 align:middle line:84%
But once the thing
is in the CCP,

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:41.380 align:middle line:84%
you've got both the
125 and the 100.

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:43.390 align:middle line:84%
And you can net those
two positions together.

00:07:43.390 --> 00:07:45.010 align:middle line:84%
And so you can net
that down and say,

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:48.320 align:middle line:84%
well, now, instead of
having 100 plus 125,

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:51.470 align:middle line:90%
I've only got 25 as my risk.

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:53.450 align:middle line:84%
And the same story
for all the others.

00:07:53.450 --> 00:07:57.160 align:middle line:84%
So in the case of B, when we
had exactly equal and offsetting

00:07:57.160 --> 00:08:00.620 align:middle line:84%
positions, B's ended up with
no counterparty risk at all.

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:02.750 align:middle line:84%
So B's in a very,
very happy situation.

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:06.520 align:middle line:84%
And everyone else is slightly
happier than they were before.

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:11.382 align:middle line:84%
There are other advantages
of CCPs, which I'm not really

00:08:11.382 --> 00:08:12.590 align:middle line:90%
going to talk about too much.

00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:15.790 align:middle line:84%
But the thing to know
is that some derivatives

00:08:15.790 --> 00:08:19.010 align:middle line:90%
are mandated to clear at CCP.

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:20.350 align:middle line:90%
So interest rate swaps--

00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:23.170 align:middle line:84%
pretty much if you have a
standard vanilla interest rate

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:25.330 align:middle line:90%
swap, you have to do this.

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:27.370 align:middle line:90%
You don't get a choice.

00:08:27.370 --> 00:08:29.830 align:middle line:84%
But more exotic trades like,
say, an interest rate swap

00:08:29.830 --> 00:08:33.370 align:middle line:84%
or something-- an
interest rate swaption,

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:37.429 align:middle line:84%
they are not eligible
to be cleared at a CCP.

00:08:37.429 --> 00:08:38.090 align:middle line:90%
So some are.

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:38.809 align:middle line:90%
Some are not.

00:08:38.809 --> 00:08:41.650 align:middle line:84%
So what you end up
with is a portfolio

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:43.570 align:middle line:84%
with some bilateral
trades versus a bunch

00:08:43.570 --> 00:08:47.440 align:middle line:84%
of different counterparties
and some trades at the CCP.

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:51.470 align:middle line:84%
So almost everybody will end
up with a mixture of stuff.

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:56.616 align:middle line:90%


00:08:56.616 --> 00:09:01.120 align:middle line:84%
So now we're going to talk about
quantitative risk measures.

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:04.080 align:middle line:84%
So what you want from a risk
measure, or any general risk

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:07.590 align:middle line:84%
measure, it should
quantify somewhat

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:10.720 align:middle line:84%
the size of the total risk or
the size of the total impact.

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:12.880 align:middle line:84%
How much money could
you possibly lose?

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:16.200 align:middle line:84%
And it should also
quantify the probability

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:20.320 align:middle line:90%
of losing that amount.

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:23.880 align:middle line:84%
So there's two aspects to it--
the size and the probability.

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:29.100 align:middle line:84%
So in 1999, there's
a paper by Artzner--

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:32.100 align:middle line:84%
there's a reference
at the back--

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:37.770 align:middle line:84%
came up with these properties
of a risk function, which

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:42.380 align:middle line:84%
is nice properties, which
are known as a coherent risk

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:46.500 align:middle line:84%
function if it satisfies
those five properties.

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:50.420 align:middle line:84%
Mostly, they say things
like the function

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:52.050 align:middle line:90%
behaves kind of normally.

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:59.720 align:middle line:84%
We'll come back-- so the
important one is subadditivity.

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:03.540 align:middle line:84%
Anyone heard of
subadditivity as a thing?

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:07.130 align:middle line:84%
So basically, what that says
is if I have two portfolios

00:10:07.130 --> 00:10:11.580 align:middle line:84%
X1 and X2, and I've got some
risk on X1 and some risk on X2,

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:13.920 align:middle line:84%
and then I bash those
two portfolios together,

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:16.550 align:middle line:84%
and now I've got a combined
portfolio, the risk

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:19.370 align:middle line:84%
on my combined portfolio,
according to my risk measure,

00:10:19.370 --> 00:10:23.150 align:middle line:84%
ought to be less than or
equal to the sum of the risk

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:25.290 align:middle line:84%
on the two portfolios I
had in the first place,

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:26.930 align:middle line:90%
which seems kind of sensible.

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:29.150 align:middle line:84%
If I put some stuff
here and some stuff here

00:10:29.150 --> 00:10:31.190 align:middle line:84%
and put it together,
it seems unlikely

00:10:31.190 --> 00:10:34.560 align:middle line:84%
that I've manufactured some
risk out of doing this.

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:36.805 align:middle line:84%
But now conversely, it
seems highly plausible

00:10:36.805 --> 00:10:38.930 align:middle line:84%
that there might be some
diversification of putting

00:10:38.930 --> 00:10:40.110 align:middle line:90%
these two things together.

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:42.630 align:middle line:84%
And so my overall risk
might well be smaller.

00:10:42.630 --> 00:10:45.860 align:middle line:84%
It seems a very plausible
and reasonable property

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:50.095 align:middle line:90%
to ask for from a risk measure.

00:10:50.095 --> 00:10:54.099 align:middle line:90%


00:10:54.099 --> 00:10:57.000 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to talk about
two risk measures in particular,

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:00.050 align:middle line:84%
which are probably the two
most common ones for looking

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:01.290 align:middle line:90%
at a whole portfolio.

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:04.160 align:middle line:84%
One is Value at Risk,
which I probably

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:06.860 align:middle line:84%
will keep referring to
as VaR, and the other

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:08.280 align:middle line:90%
is expected shortfall.

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:11.970 align:middle line:84%
So value at risk is
this amount here.

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:13.280 align:middle line:90%
It says, if I've got--

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:17.630 align:middle line:90%
it says, what is the maximum--

00:11:17.630 --> 00:11:21.650 align:middle line:84%
for a given confidence level,
what is the maximum amount

00:11:21.650 --> 00:11:26.280 align:middle line:84%
that I can say that I will
lose more than that amount.

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:27.460 align:middle line:90%
So for example.

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:30.170 align:middle line:90%


00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:35.840 align:middle line:84%
can I be 99% certain that I
won't lose more than $10 million

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:38.270 align:middle line:90%
over, say, the next 10 days?

00:11:38.270 --> 00:11:41.810 align:middle line:84%
So I have a confidence
level is called beta.

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:45.180 align:middle line:84%
There's a time horizon, which
we'll talk about in a sec.

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:49.850 align:middle line:84%
And the VaR is how
much money can I

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:54.110 align:middle line:84%
be 99% or beta percent
confident that I won't lose more

00:11:54.110 --> 00:11:55.220 align:middle line:90%
than this amount?

00:11:55.220 --> 00:12:00.350 align:middle line:84%
The expected shortfall
says, conditional that I've

00:12:00.350 --> 00:12:06.120 align:middle line:84%
exceeded that amount of loss,
so given I'm in the 1% tail

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:10.740 align:middle line:84%
if beta is 99%, or the
5% tail if beta is 95%,

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:14.870 align:middle line:84%
if I'm in that tail, what
is my expected loss, given

00:12:14.870 --> 00:12:17.220 align:middle line:90%
that I've exceeded VaR?

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:19.920 align:middle line:84%
So that's what
expected shortfall is.

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:21.830 align:middle line:84%
So hopefully, it's
indicated on here

00:12:21.830 --> 00:12:23.190 align:middle line:90%
that this is the percentage.

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:26.610 align:middle line:84%
This dark area here
is 1 minus beta.

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:29.070 align:middle line:90%
So this is, say, 1% or minus 5%.

00:12:29.070 --> 00:12:35.160 align:middle line:84%
The VaR is the biggest amount
before I get into the tail.

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:38.540 align:middle line:84%
And the expected shortfall is
the average value of that tail.

00:12:38.540 --> 00:12:41.400 align:middle line:90%


00:12:41.400 --> 00:12:44.140 align:middle line:84%
Clearly, the expected shortfall
should be bigger than the VaR.

00:12:44.140 --> 00:12:48.860 align:middle line:84%
Hopefully, that's obvious for
the same confidence level.

00:12:48.860 --> 00:12:51.840 align:middle line:90%


00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:59.747 align:middle line:84%
So VaR was kind of popularized
by JPMorgan in about the 1990s,

00:12:59.747 --> 00:13:00.580 align:middle line:90%
something like that.

00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:03.515 align:middle line:84%
Expected shortfall
came around about 2000.

00:13:03.515 --> 00:13:04.890 align:middle line:84%
And both of them
are heavily used

00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:08.670 align:middle line:84%
by regulators and lots and lots
of counterparty risk measures

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:11.190 align:middle line:90%
today.

00:13:11.190 --> 00:13:15.810 align:middle line:84%
VaR is probably the most
well-known one and most common.

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:17.560 align:middle line:90%
It's got a couple of problems.

00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:22.770 align:middle line:84%
The first problem is if you have
these kind of weird fat tail

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:24.100 align:middle line:90%
probabilities.

00:13:24.100 --> 00:13:28.530 align:middle line:84%
So you've got two probability
distributions, red and blue.

00:13:28.530 --> 00:13:31.600 align:middle line:84%
The blue has this weird
fat tail bit over here.

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:35.020 align:middle line:84%
And we'll just say, well,
the area under the blue bit

00:13:35.020 --> 00:13:36.260 align:middle line:90%
and the red bit is the same.

00:13:36.260 --> 00:13:39.530 align:middle line:84%
So both these distributions
have the same VaR

00:13:39.530 --> 00:13:42.352 align:middle line:90%
because the area up to here--

00:13:42.352 --> 00:13:44.060 align:middle line:84%
assume there's a little
bit of blue here.

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:47.870 align:middle line:84%
The area up to here is the
same as the area under that.

00:13:47.870 --> 00:13:50.840 align:middle line:84%
So both these distributions
have the same VaR.

00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:54.460 align:middle line:84%
But clearly, the blue one
has a much bigger probability

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:56.810 align:middle line:84%
of doing some fairly
horrific loss.

00:13:56.810 --> 00:14:00.340 align:middle line:84%
And also, hopefully
clearly, the blue one

00:14:00.340 --> 00:14:02.380 align:middle line:84%
would have a much bigger
expected shortfall

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:04.600 align:middle line:84%
than the red one because
the average of the blue one

00:14:04.600 --> 00:14:05.510 align:middle line:90%
is about here.

00:14:05.510 --> 00:14:07.280 align:middle line:84%
The average of the
red one is about here.

00:14:07.280 --> 00:14:10.390 align:middle line:84%
So VaR doesn't distinguish
between those two

00:14:10.390 --> 00:14:11.510 align:middle line:90%
different cases.

00:14:11.510 --> 00:14:13.870 align:middle line:84%
And you might say,
well, this is ridiculous

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:16.210 align:middle line:84%
because nobody's
going to have a-- in

00:14:16.210 --> 00:14:18.160 align:middle line:84%
real life that kind
of distribution

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:19.310 align:middle line:90%
can't possibly exist.

00:14:19.310 --> 00:14:23.950 align:middle line:84%
But that is not completely
ridiculous distribution.

00:14:23.950 --> 00:14:27.970 align:middle line:84%
It's not totally
uncommon trading policy

00:14:27.970 --> 00:14:30.710 align:middle line:84%
to sell a whole bunch
out of the money options.

00:14:30.710 --> 00:14:32.960 align:middle line:84%
And if you sell a bunch out
of the money options, what

00:14:32.960 --> 00:14:34.710 align:middle line:84%
most of the time going
to happen is you're

00:14:34.710 --> 00:14:37.310 align:middle line:84%
going to take a small profit
from the sale of those options.

00:14:37.310 --> 00:14:40.460 align:middle line:84%
And most of the time, they will
expire still out of the money.

00:14:40.460 --> 00:14:42.980 align:middle line:84%
They won't get exercised, and
you're going to take a profit.

00:14:42.980 --> 00:14:46.250 align:middle line:84%
But some of the time the
market will move enough.

00:14:46.250 --> 00:14:49.000 align:middle line:84%
And if it moves enough,
they'll get exercised,

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:51.710 align:middle line:84%
and you're going to lose a
lot of money on those options.

00:14:51.710 --> 00:14:53.740 align:middle line:84%
So that-- even
though I did contrive

00:14:53.740 --> 00:14:55.780 align:middle line:84%
that for the purposes
of this lecture,

00:14:55.780 --> 00:14:58.630 align:middle line:84%
that kind of distribution
is not as ridiculous

00:14:58.630 --> 00:15:01.720 align:middle line:90%
as you might think.

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:03.500 align:middle line:90%
So that's the first problem.

00:15:03.500 --> 00:15:07.630 align:middle line:84%
The second related problem
is that value at risk

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:09.890 align:middle line:90%
is not subadditive.

00:15:09.890 --> 00:15:14.800 align:middle line:84%
So this, again, a little
bit of a contrived example

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:18.790 align:middle line:84%
to show that if you
take, say, the 95% VaR.

00:15:18.790 --> 00:15:21.880 align:middle line:84%
I take two portfolios A
and B, with those kind

00:15:21.880 --> 00:15:24.434 align:middle line:90%
of probability of happening.

00:15:24.434 --> 00:15:27.980 align:middle line:84%
A and B by themselves,
the VaR, is zero

00:15:27.980 --> 00:15:31.030 align:middle line:84%
because there's a 96% chance
I'm going to lose nothing.

00:15:31.030 --> 00:15:34.450 align:middle line:84%
So 95% the VaR is
going to be zero.

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:37.010 align:middle line:84%
The expected shortfall
of those things is given.

00:15:37.010 --> 00:15:39.850 align:middle line:90%
I'm in that last 5% tail.

00:15:39.850 --> 00:15:44.080 align:middle line:84%
1% of the 5, I'm going to
lose nothing and 4% of the 5,

00:15:44.080 --> 00:15:45.230 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to lose 100.

00:15:45.230 --> 00:15:46.580 align:middle line:90%
So I get to 80.

00:15:46.580 --> 00:15:49.240 align:middle line:84%
And if you do the maths and
just add the two things up,

00:15:49.240 --> 00:15:51.490 align:middle line:84%
and you do the A plus
B, you can discover

00:15:51.490 --> 00:15:53.740 align:middle line:84%
that the VaR of the
combined portfolio is 100,

00:15:53.740 --> 00:15:57.100 align:middle line:84%
and the expected
shortfall is 103.

00:15:57.100 --> 00:15:59.620 align:middle line:84%
So that is a kind of
example that shows

00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:03.995 align:middle line:90%
that VaR is not subadditive.

00:16:03.995 --> 00:16:05.620 align:middle line:84%
Expected short--
obviously, this is not

00:16:05.620 --> 00:16:07.880 align:middle line:84%
proving that expected
shortfall is subadditive.

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:09.340 align:middle line:90%
It is, in this case.

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:12.940 align:middle line:84%
It's quite hard-ish to prove
that expected shortfall is

00:16:12.940 --> 00:16:13.790 align:middle line:90%
subadditive.

00:16:13.790 --> 00:16:14.780 align:middle line:90%
It is.

00:16:14.780 --> 00:16:17.170 align:middle line:84%
And I put one of the
things at the end

00:16:17.170 --> 00:16:19.970 align:middle line:84%
is seven different proofs
that it is subadditive.

00:16:19.970 --> 00:16:25.570 align:middle line:84%
So you can pick your favorite
one from the thing at the end.

00:16:25.570 --> 00:16:27.470 align:middle line:84%
I also put an
example at the end--

00:16:27.470 --> 00:16:31.630 align:middle line:84%
so even though this one is
a very contrived example.

00:16:31.630 --> 00:16:37.750 align:middle line:84%
So in real life, most things are
kind of normal or normal-like.

00:16:37.750 --> 00:16:41.150 align:middle line:84%
And in that case,
VaR is subadditive.

00:16:41.150 --> 00:16:45.400 align:middle line:84%
So in real-life situations,
this is not as much of a problem

00:16:45.400 --> 00:16:46.818 align:middle line:90%
as you might think.

00:16:46.818 --> 00:16:48.110 align:middle line:90%
And there's a paper at the end.

00:16:48.110 --> 00:16:51.080 align:middle line:84%
It talks also a lot
about that and says,

00:16:51.080 --> 00:16:53.110 align:middle line:84%
actually, lots of
people criticize VaR

00:16:53.110 --> 00:16:55.940 align:middle line:84%
because they come up with
these kind of crazy examples,

00:16:55.940 --> 00:16:57.940 align:middle line:90%
but it's not too bad.

00:16:57.940 --> 00:17:03.170 align:middle line:84%
For our purposes, given we want
to go on to optimizing stuff,

00:17:03.170 --> 00:17:04.960 align:middle line:90%
it is critically bad.

00:17:04.960 --> 00:17:09.010 align:middle line:84%
Because subadditivity--
we'll come back to this

00:17:09.010 --> 00:17:12.770 align:middle line:84%
again in a sec-- is basically
the same thing as convexity.

00:17:12.770 --> 00:17:16.300 align:middle line:84%
So not being subadditive
means VaR is not really

00:17:16.300 --> 00:17:18.680 align:middle line:90%
a convex function, in general.

00:17:18.680 --> 00:17:21.760 align:middle line:84%
And convex functions
are way, way, way easier

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:24.410 align:middle line:84%
to optimize than
nonconvex functions.

00:17:24.410 --> 00:17:27.060 align:middle line:84%
So even though
it's not a problem

00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:28.540 align:middle line:90%
if you just want to know VaR.

00:17:28.540 --> 00:17:31.710 align:middle line:84%
It is a massive problem
if you want to optimize

00:17:31.710 --> 00:17:33.530 align:middle line:90%
one of these things.

00:17:33.530 --> 00:17:37.115 align:middle line:84%
And we'll come back to
this again in a little bit.

00:17:37.115 --> 00:17:39.773 align:middle line:90%


00:17:39.773 --> 00:17:41.190 align:middle line:84%
I just wanted to
quickly point out

00:17:41.190 --> 00:17:43.260 align:middle line:84%
that there's lots and lots
of other risk measures

00:17:43.260 --> 00:17:45.600 align:middle line:90%
you can possibly choose here.

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:47.340 align:middle line:84%
VaR and expected
shortfall just happened

00:17:47.340 --> 00:17:49.470 align:middle line:90%
to be the two common ones.

00:17:49.470 --> 00:17:53.760 align:middle line:84%
You can characterize a risk
measure by how much weight

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:55.900 align:middle line:84%
it puts on a
particular percentile.

00:17:55.900 --> 00:17:59.050 align:middle line:84%
So VaR is putting all of its
weight on the beta percentile.

00:17:59.050 --> 00:18:00.840 align:middle line:84%
Expected shortfall
puts zero on everything

00:18:00.840 --> 00:18:04.080 align:middle line:84%
less than beta and averages
out everything above beta.

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:06.840 align:middle line:84%
But you could say,
well, both of those--

00:18:06.840 --> 00:18:09.457 align:middle line:84%
you can make some
criticisms of them.

00:18:09.457 --> 00:18:11.790 align:middle line:84%
You could say, well, I should
put a little bit of weight

00:18:11.790 --> 00:18:14.123 align:middle line:84%
on something that's a bit
less than the beta percentage,

00:18:14.123 --> 00:18:15.330 align:middle line:90%
and I should weight the--

00:18:15.330 --> 00:18:18.490 align:middle line:84%
the really, really
extreme tail losses,

00:18:18.490 --> 00:18:22.660 align:middle line:84%
I should weight them more than
the just beyond the tail losses.

00:18:22.660 --> 00:18:26.940 align:middle line:84%
So you can imagine having
these exponential spectral risk

00:18:26.940 --> 00:18:29.940 align:middle line:84%
functions that have these kind
of functions, which do exactly

00:18:29.940 --> 00:18:32.430 align:middle line:84%
those things, that they
put an awful lot of weight

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:38.620 align:middle line:84%
on the really bad losses at
the 99% and 99.9% things.

00:18:38.620 --> 00:18:41.920 align:middle line:90%
And it scales off going down.

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:45.390 align:middle line:84%
It's just to point out there's
a few different choices

00:18:45.390 --> 00:18:46.175 align:middle line:90%
you can make here.

00:18:46.175 --> 00:18:51.084 align:middle line:90%


00:18:51.084 --> 00:18:53.580 align:middle line:84%
So both VaR and expected
shortfall, they've

00:18:53.580 --> 00:18:55.115 align:middle line:90%
basically got two parameters.

00:18:55.115 --> 00:18:56.490 align:middle line:84%
They've got the
confidence level,

00:18:56.490 --> 00:18:59.460 align:middle line:84%
and they've got
the time horizon.

00:18:59.460 --> 00:19:05.860 align:middle line:84%
So we'll talk a bit
about the time horizon.

00:19:05.860 --> 00:19:08.367 align:middle line:84%
So that means I
want to make sure

00:19:08.367 --> 00:19:10.200 align:middle line:84%
that I'm going to lose
less than this amount

00:19:10.200 --> 00:19:13.360 align:middle line:84%
of money over one day,
five days, 10 days.

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:15.010 align:middle line:90%
That's the time horizon.

00:19:15.010 --> 00:19:18.130 align:middle line:84%
How long have I got to avoid
losing this amount of money?

00:19:18.130 --> 00:19:20.970 align:middle line:84%
If you were to
make the assumption

00:19:20.970 --> 00:19:25.350 align:middle line:84%
that the change in the
portfolio-- so this delta

00:19:25.350 --> 00:19:29.670 align:middle line:84%
P is a normal distribution,
then VaR and expected shortfall

00:19:29.670 --> 00:19:32.950 align:middle line:84%
would have these
two formats here.

00:19:32.950 --> 00:19:37.740 align:middle line:90%
So they would also be normal.

00:19:37.740 --> 00:19:41.820 align:middle line:84%
And if you assume that people
make these kind of assumptions

00:19:41.820 --> 00:19:47.340 align:middle line:84%
in the thing, that the mean
change on a daily basis

00:19:47.340 --> 00:19:51.160 align:middle line:84%
is zero, which is not a
crazy kind of assumption,

00:19:51.160 --> 00:19:53.040 align:middle line:84%
then both VaR and
expected shortfall

00:19:53.040 --> 00:19:56.750 align:middle line:84%
would be proportional to
the standard deviation

00:19:56.750 --> 00:19:57.570 align:middle line:90%
with these things.

00:19:57.570 --> 00:19:58.720 align:middle line:90%
They would be normal.

00:19:58.720 --> 00:20:01.140 align:middle line:84%
And if you did that,
and you assumed

00:20:01.140 --> 00:20:05.370 align:middle line:84%
that all the daily changes
were independently--

00:20:05.370 --> 00:20:08.010 align:middle line:84%
they were all kind of
independent identical normal

00:20:08.010 --> 00:20:09.877 align:middle line:84%
distributions so you
could add them all up.

00:20:09.877 --> 00:20:11.460 align:middle line:84%
Then you would
discover that the T-day

00:20:11.460 --> 00:20:13.500 align:middle line:84%
VaR and the T-day
expected shortfall

00:20:13.500 --> 00:20:17.220 align:middle line:84%
is proportional to the square
root of the one-day VaR, which

00:20:17.220 --> 00:20:22.200 align:middle line:84%
is the normal approximation that
people make with those things.

00:20:22.200 --> 00:20:28.277 align:middle line:84%
In reality, it seems
that the losses--

00:20:28.277 --> 00:20:30.360 align:middle line:84%
the changes that you'd
have on a typical portfolio

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:33.990 align:middle line:84%
or a typical market data
are not independent.

00:20:33.990 --> 00:20:35.543 align:middle line:84%
If you lose a lot
of money one day,

00:20:35.543 --> 00:20:36.960 align:middle line:84%
it's a reasonable
chance you might

00:20:36.960 --> 00:20:38.880 align:middle line:84%
lose some money on the--
or the market might go down

00:20:38.880 --> 00:20:39.760 align:middle line:90%
on the second day.

00:20:39.760 --> 00:20:42.160 align:middle line:84%
So there's a thing
called autocorrelation,

00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:44.700 align:middle line:84%
which is basically the
correlation of a time series

00:20:44.700 --> 00:20:46.810 align:middle line:90%
with itself shifted by one day.

00:20:46.810 --> 00:20:51.010 align:middle line:84%
And so you can-- which
is what this thing here.

00:20:51.010 --> 00:20:54.600 align:middle line:84%
And if you assume that that
autocorrelation, rho, is

00:20:54.600 --> 00:21:01.380 align:middle line:84%
some number, then it
would modify the T-day VaR

00:21:01.380 --> 00:21:03.785 align:middle line:90%
for two days by this formula.

00:21:03.785 --> 00:21:05.530 align:middle line:90%
So I put in this table.

00:21:05.530 --> 00:21:09.130 align:middle line:84%
So the top row is assuming that
there's no autocorrelation.

00:21:09.130 --> 00:21:13.440 align:middle line:84%
So that's 1 root 2 root 5, root
10 and so on is the top row.

00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:16.920 align:middle line:84%
And then as you increase
the autocorrelation,

00:21:16.920 --> 00:21:21.530 align:middle line:84%
the T-day VaR, as a
function of the one-day VaR,

00:21:21.530 --> 00:21:23.090 align:middle line:90%
gets bigger and bigger.

00:21:23.090 --> 00:21:24.240 align:middle line:90%
So I did some--

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:25.950 align:middle line:84%
I'll show you some
market data in a sec.

00:21:25.950 --> 00:21:30.870 align:middle line:84%
So I took a set of observations
from the EURO-STOXX stock index.

00:21:30.870 --> 00:21:35.720 align:middle line:84%
And that suggests that the
rho, the autocorrelation,

00:21:35.720 --> 00:21:36.793 align:middle line:90%
is about 0.1.

00:21:36.793 --> 00:21:38.210 align:middle line:84%
I did some other
indices, and they

00:21:38.210 --> 00:21:42.710 align:middle line:90%
were all between 0.05 and 1.15.

00:21:42.710 --> 00:21:45.300 align:middle line:90%
So 0.1 is kind of reasonable.

00:21:45.300 --> 00:21:51.650 align:middle line:84%
And generally, people take a
10-day VaR thing as a normal

00:21:51.650 --> 00:21:52.320 align:middle line:90%
thing.

00:21:52.320 --> 00:21:59.130 align:middle line:84%
So you'd be looking at about
this number here, this 3.46.

00:21:59.130 --> 00:22:03.800 align:middle line:84%
It's about-- that means to say
that the standard approximation

00:22:03.800 --> 00:22:07.040 align:middle line:90%
of using the square root of--

00:22:07.040 --> 00:22:10.730 align:middle line:84%
saying the square root
of the one-day VAR--

00:22:10.730 --> 00:22:13.430 align:middle line:84%
sorry-- the square root
of T times the one-day VAR

00:22:13.430 --> 00:22:16.730 align:middle line:84%
is probably going to
underestimate the actual T-day

00:22:16.730 --> 00:22:22.220 align:middle line:84%
VaR by about 10% because
it's 3.46 divided by 3.16.

00:22:22.220 --> 00:22:26.090 align:middle line:84%
But nevertheless,
almost everybody

00:22:26.090 --> 00:22:32.330 align:middle line:84%
just says that the T-day VaR is
the one-day VaR times root T.

00:22:32.330 --> 00:22:35.550 align:middle line:84%
So this is where I
got the data from.

00:22:35.550 --> 00:22:39.380 align:middle line:90%
So I picked four stock indexes--

00:22:39.380 --> 00:22:42.150 align:middle line:90%
Ibex, CAC, DAX, and EURO-STOXX.

00:22:42.150 --> 00:22:45.780 align:middle line:84%
That's, if you don't know,
Spanish, French, German,

00:22:45.780 --> 00:22:50.450 align:middle line:84%
and a kind of
pan-European stock index.

00:22:50.450 --> 00:22:53.510 align:middle line:84%
So from 2006 to
2024, you can see

00:22:53.510 --> 00:22:56.430 align:middle line:84%
there's a couple of
periods of stress in here.

00:22:56.430 --> 00:23:01.730 align:middle line:84%
One in 2008 time frame,
around about here,

00:23:01.730 --> 00:23:03.620 align:middle line:84%
which was the global
financial crash,

00:23:03.620 --> 00:23:07.260 align:middle line:84%
and one in about
2020, which was COVID.

00:23:07.260 --> 00:23:12.300 align:middle line:84%
And some degree of calmness
in between those two periods.

00:23:12.300 --> 00:23:18.225 align:middle line:84%
Clearly, there's some
correlation between these things

00:23:18.225 --> 00:23:20.090 align:middle line:90%
that's there.

00:23:20.090 --> 00:23:21.652 align:middle line:84%
On a practical note,
there's a couple

00:23:21.652 --> 00:23:22.860 align:middle line:90%
of things worth pointing out.

00:23:22.860 --> 00:23:26.910 align:middle line:84%
I picked these stock indices
because they're all European,

00:23:26.910 --> 00:23:29.360 align:middle line:84%
which means I don't need
to worry about FX rates

00:23:29.360 --> 00:23:32.000 align:middle line:84%
when I'm playing
with these things.

00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:34.225 align:middle line:84%
But even though
they're European,

00:23:34.225 --> 00:23:35.600 align:middle line:84%
they're from
different countries.

00:23:35.600 --> 00:23:40.280 align:middle line:84%
And the different countries
have different holidays.

00:23:40.280 --> 00:23:41.900 align:middle line:84%
And so you have
to decide what I'm

00:23:41.900 --> 00:23:44.540 align:middle line:84%
going to do-- what you're going
to do when one of these indexes

00:23:44.540 --> 00:23:48.337 align:middle line:90%
is on holiday and not trading.

00:23:48.337 --> 00:23:50.670 align:middle line:84%
What I did here was that if
all of them were on holiday,

00:23:50.670 --> 00:23:52.770 align:middle line:90%
I'm ignoring the day completely.

00:23:52.770 --> 00:23:55.440 align:middle line:84%
And if one of them was not on
holiday, but the rest were,

00:23:55.440 --> 00:23:56.910 align:middle line:90%
I just fill forward.

00:23:56.910 --> 00:23:59.670 align:middle line:84%
And so you'd see a daily
change of zero on those.

00:23:59.670 --> 00:24:02.787 align:middle line:84%
That might not necessarily be
the most sensible thing to do.

00:24:02.787 --> 00:24:04.620 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't really make
that much difference.

00:24:04.620 --> 00:24:08.630 align:middle line:84%
But the reason I mention
it is because if you

00:24:08.630 --> 00:24:11.880 align:middle line:84%
work in this kind of
industry for that,

00:24:11.880 --> 00:24:15.190 align:middle line:84%
you end up spending a surprising
and disproportionate amount

00:24:15.190 --> 00:24:19.100 align:middle line:84%
of time dealing with both
FX and holiday calendars.

00:24:19.100 --> 00:24:21.830 align:middle line:84%
You probably don't
expect to be doing this.

00:24:21.830 --> 00:24:24.568 align:middle line:84%
But holiday
calendars-- you spend

00:24:24.568 --> 00:24:26.110 align:middle line:84%
a lot of your time
trying to work out

00:24:26.110 --> 00:24:28.235 align:middle line:84%
what are you going to do
with these kind of things?

00:24:28.235 --> 00:24:32.618 align:middle line:90%


00:24:32.618 --> 00:24:35.830 align:middle line:84%
So there's a couple of different
ways you can estimate VaR.

00:24:35.830 --> 00:24:42.950 align:middle line:84%
One is historical simulation,
and one is doing model building.

00:24:42.950 --> 00:24:45.760 align:middle line:84%
We'll have a quick
look at both of them.

00:24:45.760 --> 00:24:47.590 align:middle line:84%
So the historical
simulation, basically

00:24:47.590 --> 00:24:50.210 align:middle line:84%
is we're going to go back
and look at the time series.

00:24:50.210 --> 00:24:54.760 align:middle line:84%
We'll look at the value of each
of these indices, the CAC, DAX,

00:24:54.760 --> 00:24:58.421 align:middle line:84%
IBEX, and EURO-STOXX
on each day.

00:24:58.421 --> 00:25:00.253 align:middle line:84%
And we'll come up
with a portfolio.

00:25:00.253 --> 00:25:01.670 align:middle line:84%
So I just made up
some portfolios.

00:25:01.670 --> 00:25:04.660 align:middle line:84%
Say I'm going to have 3,000
units of CAC, 4,000 of DAX,

00:25:04.660 --> 00:25:06.220 align:middle line:90%
and so on and so on.

00:25:06.220 --> 00:25:08.210 align:middle line:84%
And we'll call that
the portfolio weight.

00:25:08.210 --> 00:25:13.630 align:middle line:84%
And so now, I can figure
out that on the day--

00:25:13.630 --> 00:25:17.260 align:middle line:84%
the change in value
on day I is going

00:25:17.260 --> 00:25:21.790 align:middle line:84%
to be the relative change in
the underlying market index.

00:25:21.790 --> 00:25:24.070 align:middle line:84%
For example, the second
row minus the first row

00:25:24.070 --> 00:25:25.900 align:middle line:84%
for the first day
and so on, multiplied

00:25:25.900 --> 00:25:28.520 align:middle line:90%
by the weight for that index.

00:25:28.520 --> 00:25:30.440 align:middle line:84%
Then just sum it up
over all the indices.

00:25:30.440 --> 00:25:33.880 align:middle line:84%
And that will give me the
change on the I-th day.

00:25:33.880 --> 00:25:38.230 align:middle line:90%


00:25:38.230 --> 00:25:41.420 align:middle line:84%
So then I can take the
distribution of those things,

00:25:41.420 --> 00:25:45.080 align:middle line:84%
and I will get something
that looks like that.

00:25:45.080 --> 00:25:48.040 align:middle line:90%
Hopefully, that's big enough.

00:25:48.040 --> 00:25:50.297 align:middle line:84%
So one thing to add
here is that when

00:25:50.297 --> 00:25:51.880 align:middle line:84%
you're doing that,
when you're looking

00:25:51.880 --> 00:25:55.990 align:middle line:84%
at these historical
simulations, you

00:25:55.990 --> 00:25:59.450 align:middle line:84%
have to decide over what period,
over what historical period,

00:25:59.450 --> 00:26:01.622 align:middle line:90%
am I going to look at?

00:26:01.622 --> 00:26:03.970 align:middle line:84%
And the main thing
to notice here

00:26:03.970 --> 00:26:07.220 align:middle line:84%
is that depending on what
historical period you look at,

00:26:07.220 --> 00:26:10.600 align:middle line:84%
it makes a massive difference
to both the VaR and the expected

00:26:10.600 --> 00:26:12.010 align:middle line:90%
shortfall.

00:26:12.010 --> 00:26:15.910 align:middle line:84%
So what normally happens, if
you normally talk about VaR,

00:26:15.910 --> 00:26:20.050 align:middle line:84%
you talk about the most
recent, say, two years.

00:26:20.050 --> 00:26:22.010 align:middle line:84%
These are all two-year
periods, by the way.

00:26:22.010 --> 00:26:25.790 align:middle line:84%
So the top left, 2022
to the end of 2023.

00:26:25.790 --> 00:26:28.250 align:middle line:84%
Given my series went
up to the end of 2023,

00:26:28.250 --> 00:26:31.720 align:middle line:84%
this top left box would
be what you would normally

00:26:31.720 --> 00:26:35.950 align:middle line:84%
put in as VaR, the most
recent 500 days, basically,

00:26:35.950 --> 00:26:37.840 align:middle line:84%
assuming there's 250
days in a year, which

00:26:37.840 --> 00:26:39.850 align:middle line:90%
is more or less true.

00:26:39.850 --> 00:26:43.100 align:middle line:84%
The ones on the right, they're
from the two stress periods--

00:26:43.100 --> 00:26:46.670 align:middle line:84%
the global financial
crisis and COVID.

00:26:46.670 --> 00:26:52.180 align:middle line:84%
And as you might imagine, if you
compute VaR based off those two

00:26:52.180 --> 00:26:54.580 align:middle line:84%
periods, you get a
much, much bigger number

00:26:54.580 --> 00:26:57.430 align:middle line:84%
than you do of either of
the calm periods, the most

00:26:57.430 --> 00:27:00.730 align:middle line:84%
recent one, 2022, or
a period in between

00:27:00.730 --> 00:27:07.720 align:middle line:84%
the global financial crisis and
COVID, 2015 to 2016 with that.

00:27:07.720 --> 00:27:10.630 align:middle line:84%
So that makes, by far and
away, the biggest difference,

00:27:10.630 --> 00:27:12.940 align:middle line:90%
which period you choose.

00:27:12.940 --> 00:27:17.242 align:middle line:84%
And because of that, regulators
have recently introduced-- well,

00:27:17.242 --> 00:27:19.450 align:middle line:84%
not that recently-- introduced
the concept of a thing

00:27:19.450 --> 00:27:20.720 align:middle line:90%
called stress VaR.

00:27:20.720 --> 00:27:23.983 align:middle line:84%
So in addition to taking
the most recent two years,

00:27:23.983 --> 00:27:26.150 align:middle line:84%
five years, 10 years,
whatever you decide to choose,

00:27:26.150 --> 00:27:31.330 align:middle line:84%
you also pick a fixed period
known to be stressful.

00:27:31.330 --> 00:27:33.070 align:middle line:84%
So people will
pick either COVID,

00:27:33.070 --> 00:27:36.560 align:middle line:84%
or they will pick the
global financial crisis.

00:27:36.560 --> 00:27:38.800 align:middle line:84%
And then that's
generally known as S-VaR,

00:27:38.800 --> 00:27:42.190 align:middle line:84%
or stressed VaR because
there's such a big variation

00:27:42.190 --> 00:27:47.000 align:middle line:90%
in these terms that are there.

00:27:47.000 --> 00:27:52.000 align:middle line:84%
And then just to break it down
in terms of what you actually

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:55.460 align:middle line:84%
did here, what I actually did
was I took all those changes--

00:27:55.460 --> 00:27:58.840 align:middle line:84%
so there was 500 changes in
each period, more or less.

00:27:58.840 --> 00:28:00.430 align:middle line:84%
I ordered them from
the biggest change

00:28:00.430 --> 00:28:03.370 align:middle line:84%
to the-- from the biggest
loss to the biggest gain,

00:28:03.370 --> 00:28:04.430 align:middle line:90%
these things here.

00:28:04.430 --> 00:28:10.110 align:middle line:84%
And then the 5th worst one--
that's the VaR, for the 99% VaR,

00:28:10.110 --> 00:28:11.380 align:middle line:90%
because that's 1%.

00:28:11.380 --> 00:28:14.820 align:middle line:84%
And the 12th worst
one, that's the 97.5.

00:28:14.820 --> 00:28:16.000 align:middle line:90%
It's not that complicated.

00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:17.050 align:middle line:90%
You just order the thing.

00:28:17.050 --> 00:28:19.740 align:middle line:84%
You take the 5th worst one, 12th
worst one, that's what VaR is.

00:28:19.740 --> 00:28:23.620 align:middle line:84%
And expected
shortfall, for the 99%,

00:28:23.620 --> 00:28:26.550 align:middle line:84%
you take the average
of the worst four.

00:28:26.550 --> 00:28:31.980 align:middle line:84%
And for the 97.5, you take
the average of the worst 11.

00:28:31.980 --> 00:28:34.153 align:middle line:90%
There is some debate about that.

00:28:34.153 --> 00:28:36.570 align:middle line:84%
Some people claim you should
take the average of the worst

00:28:36.570 --> 00:28:40.680 align:middle line:84%
five, so including the 320, and
the average of the worst 12.

00:28:40.680 --> 00:28:44.410 align:middle line:84%
I think standard market
convention is to take the four.

00:28:44.410 --> 00:28:46.170 align:middle line:90%
Doesn't matter that much.

00:28:46.170 --> 00:28:50.885 align:middle line:84%
But that's all that's actually
happening here to do these.

00:28:50.885 --> 00:28:54.210 align:middle line:90%


00:28:54.210 --> 00:28:57.030 align:middle line:90%
All OK so far?

00:28:57.030 --> 00:29:02.020 align:middle line:84%
To do the model building
approach, it's kind of similar.

00:29:02.020 --> 00:29:04.770 align:middle line:84%
We just make the assumptions
that everything is normally

00:29:04.770 --> 00:29:06.140 align:middle line:90%
distributed.

00:29:06.140 --> 00:29:07.890 align:middle line:84%
The daily changes are
normally distributed

00:29:07.890 --> 00:29:13.048 align:middle line:84%
with mean zero and
a sigma I. We assume

00:29:13.048 --> 00:29:15.090 align:middle line:84%
that there's some kind of
correlation between all

00:29:15.090 --> 00:29:16.120 align:middle line:90%
the different indices.

00:29:16.120 --> 00:29:16.620 align:middle line:90%
And

00:29:16.620 --> 00:29:18.780 align:middle line:84%
So you say, well, the
sigma for the portfolio

00:29:18.780 --> 00:29:22.720 align:middle line:90%
is going to be this term here.

00:29:22.720 --> 00:29:27.591 align:middle line:84%
So there's a variance-covariance
matrix between the-- right--

00:29:27.591 --> 00:29:29.100 align:middle line:90%
that you get.

00:29:29.100 --> 00:29:32.880 align:middle line:84%
And using the assumption that
VaR and expected shortfall

00:29:32.880 --> 00:29:36.760 align:middle line:84%
are proportional
to the sigma again,

00:29:36.760 --> 00:29:39.160 align:middle line:90%
you get these two expressions.

00:29:39.160 --> 00:29:41.730 align:middle line:84%
So all you need to do for the
model-building approach is

00:29:41.730 --> 00:29:44.670 align:middle line:84%
figure out what the
variance-covariance matrix is

00:29:44.670 --> 00:29:48.400 align:middle line:84%
between these different
elements, which I computed here.

00:29:48.400 --> 00:29:52.770 align:middle line:84%
And I come up with a VaR,
and I get these two numbers--

00:29:52.770 --> 00:29:55.860 align:middle line:90%
225 and 257.

00:29:55.860 --> 00:29:57.660 align:middle line:84%
The main thing to
note about that

00:29:57.660 --> 00:30:00.960 align:middle line:84%
is that it's significantly,
significantly less

00:30:00.960 --> 00:30:03.520 align:middle line:90%
than the historical VaR.

00:30:03.520 --> 00:30:07.870 align:middle line:84%
So the 99th-- this is computed
for the 2022-2023 period.

00:30:07.870 --> 00:30:12.330 align:middle line:90%
So the historical VaR--

00:30:12.330 --> 00:30:15.550 align:middle line:84%
those equivalent numbers
were 320 and 377,

00:30:15.550 --> 00:30:20.765 align:middle line:84%
basically showing that
these normal distributions,

00:30:20.765 --> 00:30:22.390 align:middle line:84%
it's clearly not a
normal distribution.

00:30:22.390 --> 00:30:25.800 align:middle line:84%
There's a fatter tail in
there than you would expect.

00:30:25.800 --> 00:30:28.080 align:middle line:90%
But you can say, well--

00:30:28.080 --> 00:30:31.600 align:middle line:84%
you can work out, is this
an acceptable VaR model?

00:30:31.600 --> 00:30:32.650 align:middle line:90%
Yes or no.

00:30:32.650 --> 00:30:36.900 align:middle line:84%
So what you can say is
given I've got something

00:30:36.900 --> 00:30:41.070 align:middle line:84%
where I'm going to take 500
trials, and I expect to see--

00:30:41.070 --> 00:30:44.520 align:middle line:84%
and I expect the
VaR-- the actual loss

00:30:44.520 --> 00:30:47.730 align:middle line:84%
to have exceeded the VaR
1% of the time, that's

00:30:47.730 --> 00:30:49.450 align:middle line:90%
the definition of VaR.

00:30:49.450 --> 00:30:55.050 align:middle line:84%
And what is the probability that
I'm going to see 12 or more--

00:30:55.050 --> 00:30:57.460 align:middle line:84%
so there's 12
violations, by the way.

00:30:57.460 --> 00:31:03.690 align:middle line:84%
So if I take this 225 and say,
how many times did it actually--

00:31:03.690 --> 00:31:05.850 align:middle line:84%
according to that period,
how many times was it

00:31:05.850 --> 00:31:08.650 align:middle line:84%
actually-- the loss
was bigger than 225,

00:31:08.650 --> 00:31:11.170 align:middle line:84%
or there were 12
scenarios bigger than 225?

00:31:11.170 --> 00:31:14.040 align:middle line:84%
So what is the
probability that I

00:31:14.040 --> 00:31:18.030 align:middle line:84%
would see 12 or more days
out of a sample of 500 that

00:31:18.030 --> 00:31:22.780 align:middle line:90%
exceeded the VaR?

00:31:22.780 --> 00:31:26.550 align:middle line:84%
So that comes from the
binomial distribution, which is

00:31:26.550 --> 00:31:29.250 align:middle line:90%
that formula, and you get 5.2%.

00:31:29.250 --> 00:31:33.660 align:middle line:84%
And the general rule of thumb
is that if the probability is

00:31:33.660 --> 00:31:37.560 align:middle line:84%
5% or more of getting that
degree of-- that number

00:31:37.560 --> 00:31:42.850 align:middle line:84%
of violations of the VaR,
you say that the model is OK.

00:31:42.850 --> 00:31:46.195 align:middle line:84%
And if the probability
is less than 5%,

00:31:46.195 --> 00:31:47.820 align:middle line:84%
you'd probably reject
the model and say

00:31:47.820 --> 00:31:49.870 align:middle line:90%
the VaR is not a good model.

00:31:49.870 --> 00:31:53.010 align:middle line:84%
So even though the VAR
here is significantly lower

00:31:53.010 --> 00:31:56.700 align:middle line:84%
than the historical
simulation, you

00:31:56.700 --> 00:32:02.720 align:middle line:84%
would probably accept that
as an OK kind of model.

00:32:02.720 --> 00:32:08.150 align:middle line:84%
And the only other point
I wanted to make on that

00:32:08.150 --> 00:32:10.170 align:middle line:84%
is that what we've
basically done

00:32:10.170 --> 00:32:12.180 align:middle line:90%
there is a back-testing of VaR.

00:32:12.180 --> 00:32:14.330 align:middle line:84%
So we basically came up
with a model, and we said,

00:32:14.330 --> 00:32:15.360 align:middle line:90%
how good is that model?

00:32:15.360 --> 00:32:19.280 align:middle line:84%
How many times, based on
some historical simulation,

00:32:19.280 --> 00:32:23.490 align:middle line:90%
was the VaR exceeded?

00:32:23.490 --> 00:32:27.780 align:middle line:84%
So it's really, really easy
to do back-testing of VAR.

00:32:27.780 --> 00:32:31.580 align:middle line:84%
The single main reason
why people still

00:32:31.580 --> 00:32:34.070 align:middle line:84%
use VaR over
expected shortfall is

00:32:34.070 --> 00:32:38.720 align:middle line:84%
because doing this for expected
shortfall is quite tricky.

00:32:38.720 --> 00:32:40.190 align:middle line:84%
It's not even
clear what it means

00:32:40.190 --> 00:32:42.620 align:middle line:84%
to do back-testing of
expected shortfall.

00:32:42.620 --> 00:32:46.640 align:middle line:84%
Because, I mean, the
expected shortfall

00:32:46.640 --> 00:32:50.010 align:middle line:84%
is a function of the
probability distribution.

00:32:50.010 --> 00:32:51.300 align:middle line:90%
It's the mean of the tail.

00:32:51.300 --> 00:32:54.560 align:middle line:84%
You can't really compare
that with one sample

00:32:54.560 --> 00:32:56.310 align:middle line:84%
that I've drawn from
the distribution.

00:32:56.310 --> 00:32:59.570 align:middle line:84%
It's like comparing
apples with oranges.

00:32:59.570 --> 00:33:02.270 align:middle line:84%
There's a whole bunch
of debate around

00:33:02.270 --> 00:33:06.710 align:middle line:84%
whether back-testing expected
shortfall is flat-out

00:33:06.710 --> 00:33:09.630 align:middle line:90%
impossible, difficult, or not.

00:33:09.630 --> 00:33:14.670 align:middle line:84%
And there's a bunch of papers
at the end on this as well.

00:33:14.670 --> 00:33:18.412 align:middle line:84%
But it's definitely a lot
trickier than doing it for VaR.

00:33:18.412 --> 00:33:20.120 align:middle line:84%
And that is the main
reason, people still

00:33:20.120 --> 00:33:22.033 align:middle line:84%
use VaR, despite
its shortcomings

00:33:22.033 --> 00:33:24.325 align:middle line:84%
around the subadditivity and
the handling of the tails.

00:33:24.325 --> 00:33:27.125 align:middle line:90%


00:33:27.125 --> 00:33:29.490 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to skip
over some stuff.

00:33:29.490 --> 00:33:33.020 align:middle line:84%
This is some stuff
around regulation.

00:33:33.020 --> 00:33:38.390 align:middle line:84%
And I'll just quickly
mention this slide because--

00:33:38.390 --> 00:33:41.840 align:middle line:84%
so one of the problems
with expected shortfall

00:33:41.840 --> 00:33:46.580 align:middle line:84%
is it's kind of defined
in terms of VaR,

00:33:46.580 --> 00:33:49.430 align:middle line:84%
because it's the mean
loss that you would get,

00:33:49.430 --> 00:33:52.770 align:middle line:84%
given you've exceeded
VaR in the first place.

00:33:52.770 --> 00:33:55.361 align:middle line:90%
What you can do is there's a--

00:33:55.361 --> 00:33:57.200 align:middle line:84%
this function here--
this is in the paper

00:33:57.200 --> 00:34:01.410 align:middle line:84%
by Rockafellar and Uryasev,
which is in the back.

00:34:01.410 --> 00:34:03.230 align:middle line:84%
It says, if you take
this function F, which

00:34:03.230 --> 00:34:08.030 align:middle line:84%
is this expression here,
and you try-- and then

00:34:08.030 --> 00:34:09.983 align:middle line:90%
you minimize that over alpha.

00:34:09.983 --> 00:34:11.900 align:middle line:84%
You consider this to be
a function of X, which

00:34:11.900 --> 00:34:16.290 align:middle line:84%
is the portfolio, and
alpha is just a number,

00:34:16.290 --> 00:34:18.960 align:middle line:84%
and you minimize
this over alpha,

00:34:18.960 --> 00:34:21.380 align:middle line:84%
then the minimal
value of that function

00:34:21.380 --> 00:34:23.010 align:middle line:90%
is the expected shortfall.

00:34:23.010 --> 00:34:27.350 align:middle line:84%
And so you can find the expected
shortfall by taking that F

00:34:27.350 --> 00:34:28.620 align:middle line:90%
and minimizing it.

00:34:28.620 --> 00:34:31.940 align:middle line:84%
And that is basically if
you replace the integral

00:34:31.940 --> 00:34:35.340 align:middle line:84%
with a sum, that's
basically a linear problem.

00:34:35.340 --> 00:34:39.500 align:middle line:84%
And so it's relatively easy to
find the expected shortfall.

00:34:39.500 --> 00:34:44.580 align:middle line:84%
Moreover, if you minimize it
with respect to alpha and X,

00:34:44.580 --> 00:34:46.790 align:middle line:84%
now what you've
done is you've found

00:34:46.790 --> 00:34:51.739 align:middle line:84%
the portfolio with the minimal
expected shortfall, i.e.,

00:34:51.739 --> 00:34:54.270 align:middle line:84%
you have minimized the
expected shortfall,

00:34:54.270 --> 00:34:55.909 align:middle line:84%
which is basically
what we're trying

00:34:55.909 --> 00:34:59.100 align:middle line:90%
to do in this whole thing.

00:34:59.100 --> 00:35:01.320 align:middle line:84%
So you just have to
consider this function here.

00:35:01.320 --> 00:35:04.140 align:middle line:84%
You can minimize that
over X and alpha,

00:35:04.140 --> 00:35:06.030 align:middle line:84%
which is basically
a linear problem.

00:35:06.030 --> 00:35:11.540 align:middle line:84%
And that essentially is how you
minimize expected shortfall.

00:35:11.540 --> 00:35:15.450 align:middle line:84%
No such equivalent thing
exists for value at risk.

00:35:15.450 --> 00:35:18.200 align:middle line:84%
So even though expected
shortfall is trickier

00:35:18.200 --> 00:35:26.150 align:middle line:84%
to back test, it's
easier to minimize,

00:35:26.150 --> 00:35:33.780 align:middle line:84%
which is what I care about
more I'll skip over it.

00:35:33.780 --> 00:35:36.390 align:middle line:84%
So that's expected
shortfall and VaR.

00:35:36.390 --> 00:35:38.900 align:middle line:84%
So now we're going to start
talking about counterparty risk

00:35:38.900 --> 00:35:41.000 align:middle line:90%
and margin.

00:35:41.000 --> 00:35:47.400 align:middle line:84%
So if you have in the
derivative world--

00:35:47.400 --> 00:35:49.760 align:middle line:84%
so if I have A has
got two trades,

00:35:49.760 --> 00:35:53.257 align:middle line:84%
one with B, one with C.
Derivatives typically swaps,

00:35:53.257 --> 00:35:55.090 align:middle line:84%
means I'm going to
exchange a series of cash

00:35:55.090 --> 00:35:58.130 align:middle line:90%
flows between the two parties.

00:35:58.130 --> 00:35:59.650 align:middle line:84%
And typically, those
cash flows will

00:35:59.650 --> 00:36:01.483 align:middle line:84%
be exchanged, say, over
a three-month period

00:36:01.483 --> 00:36:03.110 align:middle line:90%
or six-month period.

00:36:03.110 --> 00:36:07.850 align:middle line:84%
So A, in this case, is
receiving some cash flows.

00:36:07.850 --> 00:36:10.690 align:middle line:84%
It's paying the series
Si to B and receiving Ti

00:36:10.690 --> 00:36:15.640 align:middle line:84%
from B. A is exposed to the
fact that before the next cash

00:36:15.640 --> 00:36:19.720 align:middle line:84%
flow arrives from B, B
might default and not pay

00:36:19.720 --> 00:36:20.540 align:middle line:90%
the cash flow.

00:36:20.540 --> 00:36:22.610 align:middle line:90%
And the same story for C.

00:36:22.610 --> 00:36:26.050 align:middle line:84%
So A has counterparty
risk to both B and C

00:36:26.050 --> 00:36:29.320 align:middle line:84%
because when we get to the next
cash flow date in, say, three

00:36:29.320 --> 00:36:31.580 align:middle line:84%
months time or six months
time or a year's time,

00:36:31.580 --> 00:36:34.360 align:middle line:84%
they might not pay that
cash flow that they're due.

00:36:34.360 --> 00:36:36.040 align:middle line:84%
So that's the
fundamental reason why

00:36:36.040 --> 00:36:38.710 align:middle line:84%
they have counterparty
risk for derivatives.

00:36:38.710 --> 00:36:41.170 align:middle line:84%
In order to mitigate
against the fact

00:36:41.170 --> 00:36:44.420 align:middle line:84%
that there might not
pay that cash flow,

00:36:44.420 --> 00:36:46.900 align:middle line:84%
what actually happens
is that they pay--

00:36:46.900 --> 00:36:50.440 align:middle line:84%
is that all these counterparties
exchange on a daily basis

00:36:50.440 --> 00:36:56.050 align:middle line:84%
the exchange variation
margin, which

00:36:56.050 --> 00:36:59.960 align:middle line:84%
means that-- which is equal to
the value of these derivatives.

00:36:59.960 --> 00:37:02.270 align:middle line:84%
So as the value goes up
and down of these things,

00:37:02.270 --> 00:37:05.300 align:middle line:84%
they'll be paying variation
margin to one another.

00:37:05.300 --> 00:37:08.360 align:middle line:84%
And that mitigates against
the cost of the cash flow

00:37:08.360 --> 00:37:09.522 align:middle line:90%
not getting paid.

00:37:09.522 --> 00:37:11.980 align:middle line:84%
But if they're paying variation
margin from one to another,

00:37:11.980 --> 00:37:16.770 align:middle line:84%
and then one party or other
goes bust and defaults,

00:37:16.770 --> 00:37:18.520 align:middle line:84%
then you've still got
to pay the variation

00:37:18.520 --> 00:37:20.030 align:middle line:90%
margin to the other person.

00:37:20.030 --> 00:37:23.450 align:middle line:84%
And as soon as one party goes
bust, what A would try and do--

00:37:23.450 --> 00:37:26.510 align:middle line:84%
so if C goes bust here, A would
try and replace that trade.

00:37:26.510 --> 00:37:28.270 align:middle line:84%
It would take it
some number of days

00:37:28.270 --> 00:37:30.770 align:middle line:84%
to replace that trade
with something else

00:37:30.770 --> 00:37:33.880 align:middle line:84%
or unwind that trade,
during which time

00:37:33.880 --> 00:37:35.790 align:middle line:84%
the value of that
trade might go up.

00:37:35.790 --> 00:37:37.790 align:middle line:84%
And because the value of
that trade is going up,

00:37:37.790 --> 00:37:42.260 align:middle line:84%
it's having to pay an increased
amount of variation margin to B.

00:37:42.260 --> 00:37:44.150 align:middle line:84%
And so therefore,
it incurs a loss.

00:37:44.150 --> 00:37:48.850 align:middle line:84%
And so it incurs a loss because
during the period between when

00:37:48.850 --> 00:37:51.920 align:middle line:84%
C defaults and when it
manages to replace that trade,

00:37:51.920 --> 00:37:57.340 align:middle line:84%
the value of the trade will
change or go up, basically.

00:37:57.340 --> 00:38:00.290 align:middle line:90%
And that is the loss.

00:38:00.290 --> 00:38:03.400 align:middle line:84%
So in order to mitigate
against that risk,

00:38:03.400 --> 00:38:06.370 align:middle line:90%
A will collect initial margin.

00:38:06.370 --> 00:38:08.000 align:middle line:90%
That's what initial margin is.

00:38:08.000 --> 00:38:09.880 align:middle line:84%
That's essentially
a covering you

00:38:09.880 --> 00:38:14.170 align:middle line:84%
for the risk in between
when a party defaults

00:38:14.170 --> 00:38:19.060 align:middle line:84%
and when you manage to unwind
or replace that position.

00:38:19.060 --> 00:38:23.170 align:middle line:84%
That slide just says
what I just said.

00:38:23.170 --> 00:38:25.900 align:middle line:84%
And then I have another slide,
which says the same thing again

00:38:25.900 --> 00:38:26.780 align:middle line:90%
in a picture.

00:38:26.780 --> 00:38:32.870 align:middle line:84%
So you have the variation margin
is getting paid up until here

00:38:32.870 --> 00:38:34.240 align:middle line:90%
between all these things.

00:38:34.240 --> 00:38:36.170 align:middle line:84%
You get some default
will happen here.

00:38:36.170 --> 00:38:37.712 align:middle line:84%
We don't know what's
going to happen.

00:38:37.712 --> 00:38:39.800 align:middle line:90%
The risk can go up or down.

00:38:39.800 --> 00:38:44.660 align:middle line:84%
And some loss could potentially
be incurred at this point.

00:38:44.660 --> 00:38:46.630 align:middle line:84%
This should hopefully
sound to you

00:38:46.630 --> 00:38:49.540 align:middle line:84%
a little bit like what we just
discussed with VaR and expected

00:38:49.540 --> 00:38:50.270 align:middle line:90%
shortfall.

00:38:50.270 --> 00:38:51.730 align:middle line:90%
And so initial margin--

00:38:51.730 --> 00:38:54.980 align:middle line:84%
in order-- how much initial
margin should I collect?

00:38:54.980 --> 00:38:58.842 align:middle line:84%
Well, basically it's going to
be a VaR based on how long you

00:38:58.842 --> 00:39:01.300 align:middle line:84%
think it's going to take you
to unwind that position, which

00:39:01.300 --> 00:39:02.815 align:middle line:84%
is called the margin
period of risk.

00:39:02.815 --> 00:39:05.680 align:middle line:90%


00:39:05.680 --> 00:39:07.550 align:middle line:84%
There's two kinds
of initial margin,

00:39:07.550 --> 00:39:10.510 align:middle line:84%
depending on whether your
trade is a cleared trade

00:39:10.510 --> 00:39:13.330 align:middle line:90%
or a bilateral trade.

00:39:13.330 --> 00:39:19.690 align:middle line:84%
The cleared margin is set up
by the clearing house, the CCP.

00:39:19.690 --> 00:39:22.900 align:middle line:84%
And that's always been the case
for years and years and years.

00:39:22.900 --> 00:39:26.860 align:middle line:84%
Up until 2016, people were
not paying initial margin

00:39:26.860 --> 00:39:28.600 align:middle line:90%
on bilateral trades.

00:39:28.600 --> 00:39:31.810 align:middle line:84%
That changed when ISDA came
up with their standard initial

00:39:31.810 --> 00:39:35.283 align:middle line:90%
margin, which is the form--

00:39:35.283 --> 00:39:37.700 align:middle line:84%
I've put the formula down here,
but it's kind of similar--

00:39:37.700 --> 00:39:41.170 align:middle line:84%
it's basically VaR is
what they put here.

00:39:41.170 --> 00:39:44.940 align:middle line:84%
And the only difference compared
to what we just discussed

00:39:44.940 --> 00:39:48.670 align:middle line:84%
is that because it's handling
nonlinear portfolios,

00:39:48.670 --> 00:39:51.480 align:middle line:84%
there's a gamma
term in there, which

00:39:51.480 --> 00:39:53.712 align:middle line:90%
I didn't talk about before.

00:39:53.712 --> 00:39:55.420 align:middle line:84%
And when you work it
all the way through,

00:39:55.420 --> 00:39:57.003 align:middle line:84%
you make exactly the
same assumptions,

00:39:57.003 --> 00:39:58.950 align:middle line:90%
that everything is normal.

00:39:58.950 --> 00:40:00.720 align:middle line:90%
Mean is zero.

00:40:00.720 --> 00:40:04.620 align:middle line:84%
You end up with the exact
same term we saw before.

00:40:04.620 --> 00:40:05.440 align:middle line:90%
Or you also--

00:40:05.440 --> 00:40:09.100 align:middle line:84%
SIMM assumes that it's a
10-day margin period of risk.

00:40:09.100 --> 00:40:11.770 align:middle line:84%
And it's a 99%
confidence interval.

00:40:11.770 --> 00:40:17.800 align:middle line:84%
So the 99% confidence interval,
that's the inverse normal of 99%

00:40:17.800 --> 00:40:23.440 align:middle line:84%
That's the 10-day
margin period of risk.

00:40:23.440 --> 00:40:26.928 align:middle line:84%
That's the exact same term we
looked at in the VaR before.

00:40:26.928 --> 00:40:28.470 align:middle line:84%
And then you get
two additional terms

00:40:28.470 --> 00:40:32.680 align:middle line:84%
that come from the convexity,
which is kind of similar.

00:40:32.680 --> 00:40:37.110 align:middle line:84%
The main point is that
initial margin is basically

00:40:37.110 --> 00:40:40.530 align:middle line:84%
some variant of VaR, or
potentially expected shortfall.

00:40:40.530 --> 00:40:43.410 align:middle line:90%


00:40:43.410 --> 00:40:46.650 align:middle line:84%
In real life, there's lots
and lots more simplifying

00:40:46.650 --> 00:40:52.560 align:middle line:84%
assumptions that people
make around all these things

00:40:52.560 --> 00:40:55.620 align:middle line:90%
to try and simplify stuff.

00:40:55.620 --> 00:40:57.960 align:middle line:84%
And all these kind
of correlations

00:40:57.960 --> 00:40:59.880 align:middle line:84%
between all the different
parameters-- this

00:40:59.880 --> 00:41:04.050 align:middle line:84%
covers the entire world,
all different asset classes,

00:41:04.050 --> 00:41:04.810 align:middle line:90%
by the way.

00:41:04.810 --> 00:41:08.940 align:middle line:84%
And so they get kind of
recalibrated on a yearly basis.

00:41:08.940 --> 00:41:13.230 align:middle line:84%
If you Google SIMM
COVID now, you'll

00:41:13.230 --> 00:41:15.240 align:middle line:84%
probably see quite
a lot of stuff

00:41:15.240 --> 00:41:19.320 align:middle line:84%
in there because SIMM
2.7 is about to come out

00:41:19.320 --> 00:41:21.070 align:middle line:90%
in the beginning of December.

00:41:21.070 --> 00:41:22.800 align:middle line:84%
And that'll be the
first one that's

00:41:22.800 --> 00:41:28.350 align:middle line:84%
calibrated without any of the
COVID or global financial crisis

00:41:28.350 --> 00:41:29.530 align:middle line:90%
data in there.

00:41:29.530 --> 00:41:32.280 align:middle line:84%
And what's going to happen,
what people expect to happen,

00:41:32.280 --> 00:41:34.950 align:middle line:84%
is that the initial margin
calculated by this SIMM

00:41:34.950 --> 00:41:36.900 align:middle line:84%
is going to massively
reduce by somewhere

00:41:36.900 --> 00:41:39.300 align:middle line:90%
in the region of 5% to 20%.

00:41:39.300 --> 00:41:42.030 align:middle line:84%
And what that will do
is release something

00:41:42.030 --> 00:41:46.150 align:middle line:84%
in the region of $40 billion of
margin back out into the wild.

00:41:46.150 --> 00:41:48.510 align:middle line:84%
Because today, people are
paying initial margin,

00:41:48.510 --> 00:41:50.760 align:middle line:84%
all these different things
with these high levels of--

00:41:50.760 --> 00:41:52.510 align:middle line:90%
using these high levels of VaR.

00:41:52.510 --> 00:41:55.000 align:middle line:84%
When they recalibrate in
the first week of December,

00:41:55.000 --> 00:41:56.200 align:middle line:90%
everything will come down.

00:41:56.200 --> 00:41:59.190 align:middle line:84%
Everybody will release
all the initial margin.

00:41:59.190 --> 00:42:01.320 align:middle line:84%
So there's quite a lot
of stuff around that

00:42:01.320 --> 00:42:04.710 align:middle line:90%
in the news at the moment.

00:42:04.710 --> 00:42:06.490 align:middle line:84%
That just talks about
how the structure--

00:42:06.490 --> 00:42:09.720 align:middle line:84%
you roll everything up
into one number across all

00:42:09.720 --> 00:42:13.560 align:middle line:90%
the different portfolios.

00:42:13.560 --> 00:42:16.390 align:middle line:90%
And I'll skip over that.

00:42:16.390 --> 00:42:18.280 align:middle line:90%
So that's what margin is.

00:42:18.280 --> 00:42:24.090 align:middle line:84%
So now we come on to trying to
optimize the initial margin.

00:42:24.090 --> 00:42:24.930 align:middle line:90%
I put margin here.

00:42:24.930 --> 00:42:27.100 align:middle line:90%
I mean initial margin.

00:42:27.100 --> 00:42:30.210 align:middle line:84%
So the situation that
we're thinking about

00:42:30.210 --> 00:42:32.610 align:middle line:84%
is we've got multiple different
financial participants--

00:42:32.610 --> 00:42:34.600 align:middle line:84%
A, B, C, D, E. Think
of them as banks.

00:42:34.600 --> 00:42:36.900 align:middle line:90%
And we've got--

00:42:36.900 --> 00:42:38.750 align:middle line:90%
I've put one CCP.

00:42:38.750 --> 00:42:41.910 align:middle line:84%
There could, in fact, be
multiple CCPs in the middle.

00:42:41.910 --> 00:42:43.730 align:middle line:84%
And all these
participants have got

00:42:43.730 --> 00:42:45.660 align:middle line:84%
a bunch of bilateral
trades versus each other,

00:42:45.660 --> 00:42:50.600 align:middle line:84%
and they've got a bunch
of trades versus the CCP.

00:42:50.600 --> 00:42:52.770 align:middle line:84%
That's the situation
that we've got.

00:42:52.770 --> 00:42:58.190 align:middle line:84%
And they're all posting margin
to each other in this situation.

00:42:58.190 --> 00:43:03.140 align:middle line:84%
And what we are trying
to do by optimizing stuff

00:43:03.140 --> 00:43:07.100 align:middle line:84%
is come up with a bunch
of hedge trades, which

00:43:07.100 --> 00:43:11.510 align:middle line:84%
is these X, H variables between
all the different participants

00:43:11.510 --> 00:43:15.560 align:middle line:84%
in order to try and
minimize the margin, i.e.,

00:43:15.560 --> 00:43:17.880 align:middle line:90%
the VaR, in the entire system.

00:43:17.880 --> 00:43:22.596 align:middle line:84%
That's the goal of what
we're trying to get to.

00:43:22.596 --> 00:43:26.190 align:middle line:84%
And you might say, well, why are
there no trades versus the CCP?

00:43:26.190 --> 00:43:29.210 align:middle line:84%
Well, you're ignoring
the cleared margin.

00:43:29.210 --> 00:43:31.940 align:middle line:90%
That's just a practical issue.

00:43:31.940 --> 00:43:37.740 align:middle line:84%
You can't book trades
between the CCP and a bank.

00:43:37.740 --> 00:43:40.250 align:middle line:84%
All you can do is book
bilateral trade between A

00:43:40.250 --> 00:43:42.780 align:middle line:84%
and B, A and E, all
these different things.

00:43:42.780 --> 00:43:47.120 align:middle line:84%
And depending on which trades
you choose, some of them

00:43:47.120 --> 00:43:48.780 align:middle line:90%
will be cleared or not cleared.

00:43:48.780 --> 00:43:52.430 align:middle line:84%
If you pick a trade, where the
trade type is like an interest

00:43:52.430 --> 00:43:55.320 align:middle line:84%
rate swap, then that will
be mandated to clear.

00:43:55.320 --> 00:43:58.430 align:middle line:84%
And so even though it's between
A and B, it'll get cleared.

00:43:58.430 --> 00:44:00.950 align:middle line:84%
And it will affect the
cleared margin and not

00:44:00.950 --> 00:44:05.210 align:middle line:84%
the bilateral margin That's why
there's no actual trades booked

00:44:05.210 --> 00:44:08.930 align:middle line:84%
between the CCP and
any of these people,

00:44:08.930 --> 00:44:11.030 align:middle line:84%
Because that's what
we're trying to do.

00:44:11.030 --> 00:44:14.790 align:middle line:84%
So that's the problem
we're trying to solve,

00:44:14.790 --> 00:44:17.540 align:middle line:84%
which looks like a lot
of maths, but in fact,

00:44:17.540 --> 00:44:19.020 align:middle line:90%
is not that complicated.

00:44:19.020 --> 00:44:21.290 align:middle line:84%
So what we're trying
to do, is we're

00:44:21.290 --> 00:44:24.920 align:middle line:84%
trying to minimize summed over
all of the parties, so all

00:44:24.920 --> 00:44:25.550 align:middle line:90%
of the banks--

00:44:25.550 --> 00:44:27.330 align:middle line:90%
A, B, C, D, E--

00:44:27.330 --> 00:44:29.610 align:middle line:90%
the margin of all those people.

00:44:29.610 --> 00:44:33.270 align:middle line:84%
So the margin is
made up of the SIMM,

00:44:33.270 --> 00:44:37.610 align:middle line:84%
the bilateral margin summed
over all the counterparties,

00:44:37.610 --> 00:44:41.960 align:middle line:84%
plus the cleared margin
summed over all the CCPs

00:44:41.960 --> 00:44:42.750 align:middle line:90%
that are there.

00:44:42.750 --> 00:44:45.650 align:middle line:84%
So that just says the sum of
all the margin in the system

00:44:45.650 --> 00:44:47.530 align:middle line:90%
is what I'm trying to minimize.

00:44:47.530 --> 00:44:50.030 align:middle line:84%
And we say, well, where SIMM
and the initial margin, there's

00:44:50.030 --> 00:44:53.032 align:middle line:84%
some functions of risk,
as we've discussed.

00:44:53.032 --> 00:44:54.240 align:middle line:90%
We know what the function is.

00:44:54.240 --> 00:44:57.320 align:middle line:90%
It's Var-like, let's say.

00:44:57.320 --> 00:44:59.900 align:middle line:90%
So we know that SIMM and--

00:44:59.900 --> 00:45:04.590 align:middle line:84%
oops, sorry-- initial margin
are some functions of the risk.

00:45:04.590 --> 00:45:08.300 align:middle line:84%
And we know that the risk
position that's there

00:45:08.300 --> 00:45:12.320 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the
initial risk, which

00:45:12.320 --> 00:45:16.418 align:middle line:84%
is the zero, plus the
sum over all the hedges

00:45:16.418 --> 00:45:17.460 align:middle line:90%
that we're going to book.

00:45:17.460 --> 00:45:19.470 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to propose
a bunch of trades.

00:45:19.470 --> 00:45:21.137 align:middle line:84%
So we sum over all
the hedges that we're

00:45:21.137 --> 00:45:23.930 align:middle line:84%
going to book, the
size of the hedge which

00:45:23.930 --> 00:45:26.030 align:middle line:84%
is the X, which is the
thing we're trying to find,

00:45:26.030 --> 00:45:30.320 align:middle line:84%
multiplied by the risk
of that particular hedge.

00:45:30.320 --> 00:45:33.860 align:middle line:84%
So that's how we know
what the new risk is.

00:45:33.860 --> 00:45:35.570 align:middle line:84%
And we've got a
couple of constraints

00:45:35.570 --> 00:45:38.072 align:middle line:84%
that we need to
put in the system.

00:45:38.072 --> 00:45:39.930 align:middle line:90%
This is the most important one.

00:45:39.930 --> 00:45:42.913 align:middle line:84%
This is what we call
the symmetry constraint,

00:45:42.913 --> 00:45:44.330 align:middle line:84%
even though it
looks like it looks

00:45:44.330 --> 00:45:46.020 align:middle line:90%
like an asymmetry constraint.

00:45:46.020 --> 00:45:51.810 align:middle line:84%
What this says is that if
P sells something to Q,

00:45:51.810 --> 00:45:56.910 align:middle line:84%
Q had better buy that same
thing from P. Exactly.

00:45:56.910 --> 00:45:59.700 align:middle line:84%
And this was-- when
we first set this up,

00:45:59.700 --> 00:46:02.420 align:middle line:84%
this was, in reality, the thing
that people were most worried

00:46:02.420 --> 00:46:03.500 align:middle line:90%
that we would mess up.

00:46:03.500 --> 00:46:06.470 align:middle line:84%
Because the biggest problem
that could have happened here

00:46:06.470 --> 00:46:07.910 align:middle line:90%
is that we told--

00:46:07.910 --> 00:46:09.060 align:middle line:90%
we set up a thing.

00:46:09.060 --> 00:46:13.610 align:middle line:84%
We tell a Goldman Sachs, go
and buy 100 million of stuff

00:46:13.610 --> 00:46:15.150 align:middle line:90%
from JPMorgan.

00:46:15.150 --> 00:46:19.130 align:middle line:84%
And then we tell JPMorgan,
go and sell 200 million

00:46:19.130 --> 00:46:21.780 align:middle line:90%
of stuff to Goldman Sachs.

00:46:21.780 --> 00:46:24.330 align:middle line:84%
So Goldman Sachs sees their
thing, and says buy 100 million.

00:46:24.330 --> 00:46:25.747 align:middle line:84%
They think, that
looks good to us.

00:46:25.747 --> 00:46:27.270 align:middle line:90%
We'll agree to this.

00:46:27.270 --> 00:46:30.910 align:middle line:84%
JPMorgan sees their thing, which
says, sell 200 million of stuff

00:46:30.910 --> 00:46:31.670 align:middle line:90%
to Goldman Sachs.

00:46:31.670 --> 00:46:33.890 align:middle line:84%
And JPMorgan goes,
that looks good.

00:46:33.890 --> 00:46:35.310 align:middle line:90%
We'll agree to this.

00:46:35.310 --> 00:46:37.310 align:middle line:84%
Goldman Sachs doesn't see
what we told JPMorgan,

00:46:37.310 --> 00:46:40.400 align:middle line:84%
and JPMorgan doesn't see
what we told Goldman Sachs.

00:46:40.400 --> 00:46:42.610 align:middle line:84%
And they only discover
that something's

00:46:42.610 --> 00:46:47.381 align:middle line:84%
gone horrifically wrong when
they try and do that trade.

00:46:47.381 --> 00:46:49.030 align:middle line:84%
So the reason that
doesn't happen

00:46:49.030 --> 00:46:52.000 align:middle line:84%
is because we've got this
constraint in the system that

00:46:52.000 --> 00:46:54.820 align:middle line:84%
says that, whatever Goldman
Sachs buys from JPMorgan,

00:46:54.820 --> 00:46:57.940 align:middle line:84%
JPMorgan had better sell that
exact same thing to Goldman

00:46:57.940 --> 00:46:59.272 align:middle line:90%
Sachs.

00:46:59.272 --> 00:47:02.802 align:middle line:84%
That is the most important
constraint in the whole system.

00:47:02.802 --> 00:47:05.260 align:middle line:84%
The second constraint, the big
one that we've got in there,

00:47:05.260 --> 00:47:07.690 align:middle line:90%
is this one, which says--

00:47:07.690 --> 00:47:09.260 align:middle line:90%
this is cash flow flatness.

00:47:09.260 --> 00:47:12.490 align:middle line:84%
This says that for
any party, let's say,

00:47:12.490 --> 00:47:19.220 align:middle line:84%
Goldman Sachs, if I sell
something to one party,

00:47:19.220 --> 00:47:21.610 align:middle line:84%
I'd better buy that
exact same thing

00:47:21.610 --> 00:47:24.410 align:middle line:84%
from some combination
of other people.

00:47:24.410 --> 00:47:26.450 align:middle line:84%
That's what that
constraint says.

00:47:26.450 --> 00:47:29.510 align:middle line:84%
We don't need that
to be in the system.

00:47:29.510 --> 00:47:33.080 align:middle line:84%
It's a kind of safety
valve that's in there.

00:47:33.080 --> 00:47:34.930 align:middle line:84%
The advantage of
having this in here

00:47:34.930 --> 00:47:37.900 align:middle line:84%
is it says, if I'm definitely
sure that everything

00:47:37.900 --> 00:47:40.300 align:middle line:84%
is cash flow flat
across the system,

00:47:40.300 --> 00:47:43.780 align:middle line:84%
nobody's going to make any
money or lose any money out

00:47:43.780 --> 00:47:44.680 align:middle line:90%
of the whole system.

00:47:44.680 --> 00:47:46.250 align:middle line:84%
Because I'm going
to sell something.

00:47:46.250 --> 00:47:47.792 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to sell
something to there.

00:47:47.792 --> 00:47:49.458 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to buy it
from somewhere else.

00:47:49.458 --> 00:47:51.400 align:middle line:84%
So I'm just-- all I'm
doing is shuffling risk

00:47:51.400 --> 00:47:52.820 align:middle line:90%
around the system.

00:47:52.820 --> 00:47:56.180 align:middle line:84%
You could relax that,
and you could say, well,

00:47:56.180 --> 00:47:57.710 align:middle line:84%
some parties could
make some money.

00:47:57.710 --> 00:47:58.835 align:middle line:90%
Some could lose some money.

00:47:58.835 --> 00:48:00.110 align:middle line:90%
Some could change some risk.

00:48:00.110 --> 00:48:02.060 align:middle line:84%
But it makes everything
a lot trickier.

00:48:02.060 --> 00:48:06.560 align:middle line:84%
So we basically enforce
this around the system.

00:48:06.560 --> 00:48:09.070 align:middle line:84%
And then the last constraint
is a risk constraint

00:48:09.070 --> 00:48:12.640 align:middle line:84%
which comes from the
parties themselves.

00:48:12.640 --> 00:48:16.840 align:middle line:84%
And what this says is that
Goldman Sachs or some party

00:48:16.840 --> 00:48:19.510 align:middle line:84%
might say, well, I don't
really want to go--

00:48:19.510 --> 00:48:22.330 align:middle line:84%
I might not want to go
long a particular risk

00:48:22.330 --> 00:48:25.690 align:middle line:84%
factor versus some particular
counterparty for whatever

00:48:25.690 --> 00:48:27.416 align:middle line:90%
reason.

00:48:27.416 --> 00:48:30.800 align:middle line:84%
So the first two are
system-wide constraints

00:48:30.800 --> 00:48:32.790 align:middle line:90%
that we impose on the thing.

00:48:32.790 --> 00:48:35.150 align:middle line:84%
And the last one is a
constraint which basically

00:48:35.150 --> 00:48:40.350 align:middle line:84%
says, don't change the risk too
much, which is what parties say.

00:48:40.350 --> 00:48:46.496 align:middle line:84%
So all of that maths,
that's all it's saying.

00:48:46.496 --> 00:48:48.500 align:middle line:84%
So if we look at-- it's
kind of-- if we look

00:48:48.500 --> 00:48:50.420 align:middle line:90%
at a very, very simple example.

00:48:50.420 --> 00:48:52.380 align:middle line:90%
If I take a three-party system--

00:48:52.380 --> 00:48:55.777 align:middle line:84%
A, B, C-- so this
might be a bit small--

00:48:55.777 --> 00:48:57.360 align:middle line:84%
and you put it in
there and say, well,

00:48:57.360 --> 00:48:59.820 align:middle line:84%
how could I minimize the
margin around that system?

00:48:59.820 --> 00:49:01.850 align:middle line:84%
And the way you'd
minimize it, you've

00:49:01.850 --> 00:49:04.610 align:middle line:84%
got AC's got to trade with
10, AB's got to trade with 2,

00:49:04.610 --> 00:49:07.260 align:middle line:90%
and BC has got a trade with 9.

00:49:07.260 --> 00:49:09.950 align:middle line:84%
Well, what you do is you
move the median trade

00:49:09.950 --> 00:49:12.030 align:middle line:90%
around the whole triangle.

00:49:12.030 --> 00:49:14.690 align:middle line:84%
If you move one median
trade around-- one trade

00:49:14.690 --> 00:49:17.450 align:middle line:84%
around the whole
triangle, I'm guaranteed

00:49:17.450 --> 00:49:21.230 align:middle line:84%
to satisfy by construction
those first two

00:49:21.230 --> 00:49:24.910 align:middle line:84%
constraints because I'm only
moving the exact same thing

00:49:24.910 --> 00:49:26.090 align:middle line:90%
around the whole system.

00:49:26.090 --> 00:49:30.200 align:middle line:84%
And if I'm moving, just
say in this case, 9.

00:49:30.200 --> 00:49:31.300 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to--

00:49:31.300 --> 00:49:32.960 align:middle line:90%
A is going to buy 9, sell 9.

00:49:32.960 --> 00:49:34.430 align:middle line:90%
B is going to buy 9, sell 9.

00:49:34.430 --> 00:49:35.900 align:middle line:90%
C is going to buy 9, sell 9.

00:49:35.900 --> 00:49:38.180 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to satisfy the
second constraint as well.

00:49:38.180 --> 00:49:40.990 align:middle line:84%
I've ignored the third
constraint for now.

00:49:40.990 --> 00:49:42.970 align:middle line:84%
And the reason I
picked that is--

00:49:42.970 --> 00:49:44.980 align:middle line:84%
those particular examples
with 2, 9, and 10,

00:49:44.980 --> 00:49:48.740 align:middle line:84%
is that actually if you fiddle
around with the maths a bit,

00:49:48.740 --> 00:49:53.110 align:middle line:84%
the thing you should move is the
median trade given any triangle.

00:49:53.110 --> 00:49:55.120 align:middle line:84%
And you can take any
network, and you can always

00:49:55.120 --> 00:49:57.580 align:middle line:84%
break a network into
a bunch of triangles.

00:49:57.580 --> 00:50:00.830 align:middle line:84%
And for every triangle, you
just move the median around.

00:50:00.830 --> 00:50:02.650 align:middle line:90%
That's what you should do.

00:50:02.650 --> 00:50:05.110 align:middle line:84%
The problems are this won't
satisfy all the different--

00:50:05.110 --> 00:50:07.520 align:middle line:84%
if you have additional
constraints, it causes problems.

00:50:07.520 --> 00:50:11.350 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, some
edges of this triangle

00:50:11.350 --> 00:50:13.580 align:middle line:84%
would also be edges
of other triangles.

00:50:13.580 --> 00:50:15.970 align:middle line:84%
And so you end up and you
can-- it won't generally

00:50:15.970 --> 00:50:17.902 align:middle line:90%
solve the problem.

00:50:17.902 --> 00:50:20.000 align:middle line:84%
But it's a good way
of thinking about it.

00:50:20.000 --> 00:50:22.770 align:middle line:84%
We always think about
triangles and how

00:50:22.770 --> 00:50:25.550 align:middle line:84%
you would move risk around
in a particular triangle.

00:50:25.550 --> 00:50:28.920 align:middle line:90%


00:50:28.920 --> 00:50:31.800 align:middle line:90%
So that's that.

00:50:31.800 --> 00:50:35.050 align:middle line:84%
In reality, we just use a big
numerical solver around this.

00:50:35.050 --> 00:50:36.780 align:middle line:84%
What we're in the
business here is

00:50:36.780 --> 00:50:41.670 align:middle line:84%
we're solving minimization
problems or operational research

00:50:41.670 --> 00:50:42.580 align:middle line:90%
type problems.

00:50:42.580 --> 00:50:46.830 align:middle line:84%
They're of the form minimize
F subject to some other stuff.

00:50:46.830 --> 00:50:51.090 align:middle line:84%
And as I mentioned earlier,
solving complex problems

00:50:51.090 --> 00:50:53.790 align:middle line:84%
is way, way, way easier than
solving general, nonlinear

00:50:53.790 --> 00:50:54.490 align:middle line:90%
problems.

00:50:54.490 --> 00:50:57.340 align:middle line:84%
And convexity is basically the
same thing as subadditivity.

00:50:57.340 --> 00:50:59.800 align:middle line:84%
That's the definition
of convexity.

00:50:59.800 --> 00:51:02.250 align:middle line:84%
And that should remind
you of the definition

00:51:02.250 --> 00:51:04.720 align:middle line:90%
we saw earlier of subadditivity.

00:51:04.720 --> 00:51:07.270 align:middle line:84%
So we saw earlier minimizing
expected shortfall.

00:51:07.270 --> 00:51:09.750 align:middle line:84%
That's basically a
piecewise linear problem.

00:51:09.750 --> 00:51:13.690 align:middle line:84%
All these things we're talking
about, SIMM is nearly convex.

00:51:13.690 --> 00:51:15.730 align:middle line:84%
There are different
add-ons that you can have,

00:51:15.730 --> 00:51:17.557 align:middle line:90%
which are usually convex.

00:51:17.557 --> 00:51:19.140 align:middle line:84%
And some of the maths
that we get into

00:51:19.140 --> 00:51:23.160 align:middle line:84%
is, how do you come up with
approximations of these things

00:51:23.160 --> 00:51:25.020 align:middle line:90%
which genuinely are convex.

00:51:25.020 --> 00:51:28.540 align:middle line:84%
We tried using proper nonlinear
solvers on these things.

00:51:28.540 --> 00:51:31.470 align:middle line:84%
Way easier just to approximate
the function you want and use

00:51:31.470 --> 00:51:33.190 align:middle line:90%
a convex solver on it.

00:51:33.190 --> 00:51:35.910 align:middle line:84%
Because the state of the
technology for solving convex

00:51:35.910 --> 00:51:39.300 align:middle line:84%
optimization problems is
really, really, really good.

00:51:39.300 --> 00:51:42.390 align:middle line:84%
It would be crazy to write
your own version of a convex

00:51:42.390 --> 00:51:45.150 align:middle line:84%
optimization solver because
there are loads of open source

00:51:45.150 --> 00:51:47.070 align:middle line:90%
and commercial available--

00:51:47.070 --> 00:51:49.170 align:middle line:90%
things available.

00:51:49.170 --> 00:51:50.950 align:middle line:90%
So don't do it.

00:51:50.950 --> 00:51:54.520 align:middle line:84%
Just plug it into a
system, and it works.

00:51:54.520 --> 00:51:57.480 align:middle line:84%
The thing to note is
that how difficult

00:51:57.480 --> 00:52:01.140 align:middle line:84%
it is to solve the problem is
really, really, really sensitive

00:52:01.140 --> 00:52:02.992 align:middle line:90%
on the specifics of the problem.

00:52:02.992 --> 00:52:05.200 align:middle line:84%
And that doesn't really mean
the size of the problem.

00:52:05.200 --> 00:52:08.080 align:middle line:84%
The size is not really
too much of an issue.

00:52:08.080 --> 00:52:10.620 align:middle line:84%
It's all kinds of the
structure of the problem, how

00:52:10.620 --> 00:52:11.920 align:middle line:90%
sparse is the problem.

00:52:11.920 --> 00:52:13.337 align:middle line:84%
And the thing these
are the things

00:52:13.337 --> 00:52:18.300 align:middle line:90%
that matter in the real world

00:52:18.300 --> 00:52:21.390 align:middle line:84%
Also, it's a good idea to
use modeling languages.

00:52:21.390 --> 00:52:24.130 align:middle line:84%
So there are lots of
modeling languages available.

00:52:24.130 --> 00:52:26.290 align:middle line:90%
Gurobi is the one we use.

00:52:26.290 --> 00:52:28.202 align:middle line:84%
I think you may-- people
might have used it.

00:52:28.202 --> 00:52:29.910 align:middle line:84%
I think you can get
it for free if you're

00:52:29.910 --> 00:52:33.190 align:middle line:90%
an academic institution.

00:52:33.190 --> 00:52:35.190 align:middle line:84%
In case anybody wants to
spend 10 seconds trying

00:52:35.190 --> 00:52:39.960 align:middle line:84%
to solve that in their head,
this is an integer problem,

00:52:39.960 --> 00:52:42.960 align:middle line:84%
trying to minimize 5 x1 plus
8 x2 subject to those two

00:52:42.960 --> 00:52:45.815 align:middle line:84%
constraints, you can probably
do that in your head.

00:52:45.815 --> 00:52:49.050 align:middle line:90%


00:52:49.050 --> 00:52:51.210 align:middle line:90%
Hang on.

00:52:51.210 --> 00:52:52.765 align:middle line:90%
So we did that.

00:52:52.765 --> 00:52:53.265 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:52:53.265 --> 00:52:56.850 align:middle line:90%


00:52:56.850 --> 00:52:58.680 align:middle line:90%
So we did it on Gurobi.

00:52:58.680 --> 00:53:02.370 align:middle line:84%
Using the 24 physical
cores probably

00:53:02.370 --> 00:53:05.550 align:middle line:84%
is overkill for that
problem, but it comes up

00:53:05.550 --> 00:53:07.728 align:middle line:84%
in 10 milliseconds
with the answer of 31.

00:53:07.728 --> 00:53:09.270 align:middle line:84%
Hopefully, if you
did it, that's what

00:53:09.270 --> 00:53:14.130 align:middle line:84%
you tried, just to show
how easy it is to solve

00:53:14.130 --> 00:53:16.410 align:middle line:90%
some of these problems.

00:53:16.410 --> 00:53:20.628 align:middle line:84%
In real life, a typical
problem that we would have

00:53:20.628 --> 00:53:23.910 align:middle line:84%
might have 25 different
parties in the network.

00:53:23.910 --> 00:53:28.440 align:middle line:84%
Might be, say, for an FX delta
30-odd different currencies that

00:53:28.440 --> 00:53:30.150 align:middle line:90%
we've got in there.

00:53:30.150 --> 00:53:32.140 align:middle line:84%
We pick different kinds
of hedge instruments,

00:53:32.140 --> 00:53:34.390 align:middle line:90%
so we try and pick simple ones.

00:53:34.390 --> 00:53:38.220 align:middle line:84%
The reason for picking things
like nondeliverable FX forwards

00:53:38.220 --> 00:53:40.620 align:middle line:84%
is that each individual
hedge type there

00:53:40.620 --> 00:53:43.810 align:middle line:84%
has exposure to exactly one
underlying, namely the currency.

00:53:43.810 --> 00:53:46.170 align:middle line:84%
If I pick a dollar
euro nondeliverable,

00:53:46.170 --> 00:53:48.750 align:middle line:84%
that's got exposure to the
dollar-euro FX rate and nothing

00:53:48.750 --> 00:53:52.770 align:middle line:84%
but the dollar-euro FX rate
in this case, which turns out

00:53:52.770 --> 00:53:54.120 align:middle line:90%
to be useful.

00:53:54.120 --> 00:53:57.300 align:middle line:84%
And if you do that, you end up
with a system which is something

00:53:57.300 --> 00:54:01.210 align:middle line:84%
like, say, 20,000 variables,
40,000 constraints.

00:54:01.210 --> 00:54:05.230 align:middle line:84%
Something like that is the
real size of the problem.

00:54:05.230 --> 00:54:10.480 align:middle line:84%
And really, when you're solving
convex optimization problems,

00:54:10.480 --> 00:54:12.060 align:middle line:84%
solving convex
optimization problems

00:54:12.060 --> 00:54:14.920 align:middle line:84%
is easy because the convex
problem looks like a bowl.

00:54:14.920 --> 00:54:18.180 align:middle line:90%
And so they have one minimum.

00:54:18.180 --> 00:54:21.590 align:middle line:84%
And so you can convert
the optimization problem

00:54:21.590 --> 00:54:25.040 align:middle line:84%
into a root-finding problem,
which is find where the root

00:54:25.040 --> 00:54:26.420 align:middle line:90%
of the derivative is--

00:54:26.420 --> 00:54:29.570 align:middle line:84%
where the derivative
is equal to zero.

00:54:29.570 --> 00:54:30.757 align:middle line:90%
So that's root-finding.

00:54:30.757 --> 00:54:32.840 align:middle line:84%
People generally use
something like Newton-Raphson

00:54:32.840 --> 00:54:35.030 align:middle line:90%
for root-finding.

00:54:35.030 --> 00:54:37.280 align:middle line:84%
When you use
Newton-Raphson, you've

00:54:37.280 --> 00:54:40.080 align:middle line:90%
got to compute a Hessian matrix.

00:54:40.080 --> 00:54:42.810 align:middle line:84%
And then you need to
invert that Hessian matrix.

00:54:42.810 --> 00:54:44.810 align:middle line:84%
That's the slow part
of the entire system

00:54:44.810 --> 00:54:48.450 align:middle line:84%
is that you're repeatedly
inverting Hessian matrices.

00:54:48.450 --> 00:54:52.460 align:middle line:84%
So the matrix might
be 40,000-ish.

00:54:52.460 --> 00:54:55.710 align:middle line:84%
Matrix inversion is basically
an n cubed kind of operation.

00:54:55.710 --> 00:54:58.310 align:middle line:84%
So you end up with this
unfeasibly large number

00:54:58.310 --> 00:55:00.420 align:middle line:90%
of operations to solve.

00:55:00.420 --> 00:55:04.520 align:middle line:84%
In general, most computers
will not be able to factorize

00:55:04.520 --> 00:55:12.060 align:middle line:84%
a 40,000 matrix for a randomly
chosen 40,000 by 40,000 matrix.

00:55:12.060 --> 00:55:14.780 align:middle line:84%
However, the problems
that we have here

00:55:14.780 --> 00:55:18.450 align:middle line:84%
are very much not
randomly chosen matrices.

00:55:18.450 --> 00:55:20.750 align:middle line:84%
They have an awful lot
of structure involved,

00:55:20.750 --> 00:55:23.630 align:middle line:84%
and they're massively,
massively sparse.

00:55:23.630 --> 00:55:27.920 align:middle line:84%
And most solvers, the reason
why they're very mature,

00:55:27.920 --> 00:55:31.540 align:middle line:84%
is they take advantage of all
this matrix structure that's

00:55:31.540 --> 00:55:32.040 align:middle line:90%
there.

00:55:32.040 --> 00:55:34.430 align:middle line:84%
And so they can do
way, way, way better

00:55:34.430 --> 00:55:36.090 align:middle line:90%
than you'd expect from this.

00:55:36.090 --> 00:55:40.880 align:middle line:84%
So in particular, for
FX delta, the matrix

00:55:40.880 --> 00:55:42.600 align:middle line:84%
looks a little bit
like this right.

00:55:42.600 --> 00:55:45.380 align:middle line:84%
So you've got a bit stuff at
the top of the matrix, which

00:55:45.380 --> 00:55:48.380 align:middle line:84%
is the thing I talked around
around the symmetry constraints

00:55:48.380 --> 00:55:49.740 align:middle line:90%
and the flatness constraints.

00:55:49.740 --> 00:55:52.975 align:middle line:84%
But the vast, vast
majority of the constraints

00:55:52.975 --> 00:55:55.100 align:middle line:84%
are those ones at the bottom
of that picture, which

00:55:55.100 --> 00:55:56.870 align:middle line:84%
are the risk
constraints specified

00:55:56.870 --> 00:55:59.360 align:middle line:90%
by the individual parties.

00:55:59.360 --> 00:56:03.000 align:middle line:84%
And they are guaranteed
to be block diagonal.

00:56:03.000 --> 00:56:06.540 align:middle line:84%
Because each-- if I
have a particular hedge,

00:56:06.540 --> 00:56:09.800 align:middle line:84%
say, the hedge AB,
that's only going

00:56:09.800 --> 00:56:13.500 align:middle line:84%
to affect the risk between party
A and party B at worst case.

00:56:13.500 --> 00:56:15.510 align:middle line:84%
And then the hedges
between A and C,

00:56:15.510 --> 00:56:18.720 align:middle line:84%
they're only going to affect the
risk between parties A and C.

00:56:18.720 --> 00:56:22.303 align:middle line:84%
So I'm definitely going to get a
block diagonal matrix down here.

00:56:22.303 --> 00:56:23.970 align:middle line:84%
And everything else
is going to be zero.

00:56:23.970 --> 00:56:26.010 align:middle line:84%
So the vast majority
is going to be zero.

00:56:26.010 --> 00:56:28.590 align:middle line:84%
And if I pick these
NDF trades as well,

00:56:28.590 --> 00:56:30.680 align:middle line:84%
and those SDF trades
only have exposure

00:56:30.680 --> 00:56:32.540 align:middle line:84%
to one risk factor
themselves, I'm

00:56:32.540 --> 00:56:34.855 align:middle line:84%
just going to get a straight
diagonal matrix here.

00:56:34.855 --> 00:56:36.980 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, inverting
a straight diagonal matrix

00:56:36.980 --> 00:56:38.362 align:middle line:90%
is trivially easy.

00:56:38.362 --> 00:56:40.070 align:middle line:84%
So I've got a bit at
the top, which makes

00:56:40.070 --> 00:56:41.370 align:middle line:90%
it a bit more complicated.

00:56:41.370 --> 00:56:44.510 align:middle line:84%
But it's a little bit at
the top, plus basically

00:56:44.510 --> 00:56:46.920 align:middle line:90%
a massive, big diagonal matrix.

00:56:46.920 --> 00:56:48.930 align:middle line:84%
That's called a
Dantzig-Wolfe structure,

00:56:48.930 --> 00:56:52.650 align:middle line:84%
and there are techniques
known to factorize and invert

00:56:52.650 --> 00:56:53.525 align:middle line:90%
those kind of things.

00:56:53.525 --> 00:56:57.920 align:middle line:90%


00:56:57.920 --> 00:56:59.910 align:middle line:90%
So then I like this slide.

00:56:59.910 --> 00:57:03.230 align:middle line:84%
I think this is the one slide
I've used all three years.

00:57:03.230 --> 00:57:05.150 align:middle line:84%
The problems that you
get into in real life

00:57:05.150 --> 00:57:11.967 align:middle line:84%
are not solving the big
minimization problem.

00:57:11.967 --> 00:57:13.550 align:middle line:84%
You just literally
write down the math

00:57:13.550 --> 00:57:19.830 align:middle line:84%
that you want, more or less
plug that into the solver.

00:57:19.830 --> 00:57:22.610 align:middle line:84%
But in real life,
you get situations

00:57:22.610 --> 00:57:25.110 align:middle line:84%
where you've got
nearly parallel risks.

00:57:25.110 --> 00:57:28.402 align:middle line:84%
You have two trades which
are nearly the same,

00:57:28.402 --> 00:57:31.310 align:middle line:84%
but not quite exactly the
same, or can't be represented

00:57:31.310 --> 00:57:33.870 align:middle line:90%
exactly the same on a computer.

00:57:33.870 --> 00:57:36.320 align:middle line:84%
So this example
here, if I really

00:57:36.320 --> 00:57:38.910 align:middle line:84%
wanted to solve this
first equation here,

00:57:38.910 --> 00:57:42.560 align:middle line:84%
obviously everyone-- hopefully,
you can see that essentially,

00:57:42.560 --> 00:57:46.050 align:middle line:84%
there's lots of solutions
to that right with this.

00:57:46.050 --> 00:57:49.950 align:middle line:84%
But you can't represent a
third properly on a computer.

00:57:49.950 --> 00:57:52.790 align:middle line:84%
So if you actually
tried to represent that

00:57:52.790 --> 00:57:54.720 align:middle line:84%
on a computer, what
you might end up with,

00:57:54.720 --> 00:57:58.550 align:middle line:84%
for example, instead of
putting a third, a third X1

00:57:58.550 --> 00:58:00.960 align:middle line:84%
plus a third Y1 as
your second equation,

00:58:00.960 --> 00:58:05.030 align:middle line:84%
you might end up with
this thing here 0.333 x1--

00:58:05.030 --> 00:58:09.140 align:middle line:90%
sorry-- 0.3333 x1 plus 0.333 Y1.

00:58:09.140 --> 00:58:11.510 align:middle line:90%
And that has this solution.

00:58:11.510 --> 00:58:13.120 align:middle line:90%
0, 1.

00:58:13.120 --> 00:58:16.370 align:middle line:84%
But you make a tiny, tiny
change to that thing--

00:58:16.370 --> 00:58:21.208 align:middle line:84%
so all I did was I flipped
over the solution here.

00:58:21.208 --> 00:58:23.500 align:middle line:84%
Instead of having three 3's
at the end, having four 3's

00:58:23.500 --> 00:58:24.290 align:middle line:90%
at the end.

00:58:24.290 --> 00:58:26.060 align:middle line:84%
And instead of getting
a solution 0, 1,

00:58:26.060 --> 00:58:28.150 align:middle line:90%
I've now got a solution 1, 0.

00:58:28.150 --> 00:58:32.140 align:middle line:84%
And if I make another small
change to those things,

00:58:32.140 --> 00:58:33.130 align:middle line:90%
it says instead of--

00:58:33.130 --> 00:58:35.210 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to get 0.3.

00:58:35.210 --> 00:58:40.180 align:middle line:84%
Then that has a single
solution of minus 110, 111.

00:58:40.180 --> 00:58:43.670 align:middle line:84%
That's the solution
to that problem.

00:58:43.670 --> 00:58:45.830 align:middle line:84%
Now, it's highly plausible
I might also say, well,

00:58:45.830 --> 00:58:47.778 align:middle line:84%
I want all these
X's to be positive.

00:58:47.778 --> 00:58:49.070 align:middle line:90%
That might be a thing in there.

00:58:49.070 --> 00:58:52.040 align:middle line:84%
And so entirely due
to numerical noise,

00:58:52.040 --> 00:58:53.870 align:middle line:84%
it will say if I want
X to be positive,

00:58:53.870 --> 00:58:57.907 align:middle line:84%
and the only solution to this
problem is minus 110, 111,

00:58:57.907 --> 00:58:59.990 align:middle line:84%
any system is going to go,
well, that's infeasible

00:58:59.990 --> 00:59:01.670 align:middle line:84%
and so it will
refuse to solve it.

00:59:01.670 --> 00:59:05.290 align:middle line:84%
And so the problems that
we get into real life

00:59:05.290 --> 00:59:06.940 align:middle line:84%
is trying to
understand these things

00:59:06.940 --> 00:59:10.210 align:middle line:84%
and coming up with techniques to
remove these kind of situations

00:59:10.210 --> 00:59:15.070 align:middle line:84%
from the system,
rather than trying

00:59:15.070 --> 00:59:18.460 align:middle line:84%
to actually solve
convex optimization

00:59:18.460 --> 00:59:22.390 align:middle line:90%
problems in their general form.

00:59:22.390 --> 00:59:23.947 align:middle line:90%
That's there.

00:59:23.947 --> 00:59:25.780 align:middle line:84%
This is the same thing
as before, except now

00:59:25.780 --> 00:59:29.150 align:middle line:84%
for the FX delta, the
full FX delta problem,

00:59:29.150 --> 00:59:31.240 align:middle line:90%
we're up to 48 cores now.

00:59:31.240 --> 00:59:34.726 align:middle line:84%
And you can see here,
it really was 114,000--

00:59:34.726 --> 00:59:38.410 align:middle line:84%
it was 100,000 by 100,000 matrix
is what we ended up trying

00:59:38.410 --> 00:59:39.970 align:middle line:90%
to solve.

00:59:39.970 --> 00:59:44.620 align:middle line:84%
It's worth noting this presolve
thing here removed close to half

00:59:44.620 --> 00:59:45.140 align:middle line:90%
of it.

00:59:45.140 --> 00:59:47.230 align:middle line:84%
What presolve is,
is sometimes you

00:59:47.230 --> 00:59:48.730 align:middle line:84%
get all these
different constraints,

00:59:48.730 --> 00:59:49.855 align:middle line:90%
and you have them in there.

00:59:49.855 --> 00:59:52.970 align:middle line:84%
And you end up with a constraint
that says X1 is greater than 1,

00:59:52.970 --> 00:59:56.205 align:middle line:84%
and another one that says
X1 is greater than 2.

00:59:56.205 --> 00:59:58.330 align:middle line:84%
So you might as well chuck
out the first constraint

00:59:58.330 --> 01:00:00.955 align:middle line:84%
and say, well, if X1 is greater
than 1 and it's greater than 2,

01:00:00.955 --> 01:00:02.560 align:middle line:84%
chuck out the first
one, and just keep

01:00:02.560 --> 01:00:04.990 align:middle line:84%
the X1 is greater
than 2 constraint.

01:00:04.990 --> 01:00:06.260 align:middle line:90%
And that happens a lot.

01:00:06.260 --> 01:00:09.730 align:middle line:84%
And so you can massively
reduce the size.

01:00:09.730 --> 01:00:12.790 align:middle line:84%
And so we get down
to a thing like this.

01:00:12.790 --> 01:00:14.510 align:middle line:84%
But you can see
even this problem,

01:00:14.510 --> 01:00:19.900 align:middle line:84%
you can solve it in 11
seconds and with very, very

01:00:19.900 --> 01:00:21.550 align:middle line:84%
few iterations,
which is basically

01:00:21.550 --> 01:00:26.410 align:middle line:84%
a testament to the amazing power
of Newton-Raphson quadratic

01:00:26.410 --> 01:00:29.320 align:middle line:90%
convergence.

01:00:29.320 --> 01:00:32.860 align:middle line:84%
So what happens when
you try and solve it?

01:00:32.860 --> 01:00:35.202 align:middle line:84%
This is how you have
the initial risk,

01:00:35.202 --> 01:00:37.660 align:middle line:84%
and this is with the optimized
risk on the right-hand side.

01:00:37.660 --> 01:00:40.220 align:middle line:84%
As you might expect,
it tries to form--

01:00:40.220 --> 01:00:41.770 align:middle line:84%
it tries to basically
get it, but I

01:00:41.770 --> 01:00:44.240 align:middle line:84%
don't have some positive numbers
and some negative numbers.

01:00:44.240 --> 01:00:45.980 align:middle line:84%
It tries to nestle
the risk together.

01:00:45.980 --> 01:00:48.420 align:middle line:90%
So what you can see here.

01:00:48.420 --> 01:00:51.040 align:middle line:90%


01:00:51.040 --> 01:00:54.320 align:middle line:84%
You try this on a
20-party system,

01:00:54.320 --> 01:00:56.150 align:middle line:84%
again just removing
all the constraints.

01:00:56.150 --> 01:00:58.960 align:middle line:84%
Again, it can remove
almost all the margin

01:00:58.960 --> 01:01:00.350 align:middle line:90%
between the different systems.

01:01:00.350 --> 01:01:01.780 align:middle line:90%
And you end up with either--

01:01:01.780 --> 01:01:05.240 align:middle line:84%
for any given row, either
pink stuff or green stuff.

01:01:05.240 --> 01:01:06.710 align:middle line:84%
It's either positive
or negative.

01:01:06.710 --> 01:01:09.190 align:middle line:84%
But you don't have pink and
green stuff in the same row

01:01:09.190 --> 01:01:10.412 align:middle line:90%
or in the same column.

01:01:10.412 --> 01:01:13.120 align:middle line:84%
And what that says is it managed
to get rid of all the situations

01:01:13.120 --> 01:01:13.840 align:middle line:90%
where I was up.

01:01:13.840 --> 01:01:15.890 align:middle line:90%
I was up here and down there.

01:01:15.890 --> 01:01:18.158 align:middle line:90%
I just want to be up or down.

01:01:18.158 --> 01:01:19.700 align:middle line:84%
So I've netted all
the risk together.

01:01:19.700 --> 01:01:26.530 align:middle line:84%
That's what the system was
basically trying to do.

01:01:26.530 --> 01:01:28.720 align:middle line:84%
You try it on a real system
with all the constraints

01:01:28.720 --> 01:01:30.610 align:middle line:84%
and stuff like that,
it's less convincing

01:01:30.610 --> 01:01:35.410 align:middle line:84%
what it was trying to do because
the constraints hold this stuff

01:01:35.410 --> 01:01:39.880 align:middle line:90%
in check here with their--

01:01:39.880 --> 01:01:42.250 align:middle line:84%
Just out of interest,
this kind of diagonal

01:01:42.250 --> 01:01:44.830 align:middle line:84%
line down the front,
that's essentially

01:01:44.830 --> 01:01:47.202 align:middle line:90%
the margin versus itself.

01:01:47.202 --> 01:01:49.160 align:middle line:84%
So you obviously cannot
pay margin to yourself,

01:01:49.160 --> 01:01:54.970 align:middle line:84%
which is why you get a kind of
blank line down the diagonal.

01:01:54.970 --> 01:01:57.530 align:middle line:84%
And the square stuff
in the middle--

01:01:57.530 --> 01:02:00.060 align:middle line:84%
I originally thought
that was some bug.

01:02:00.060 --> 01:02:03.900 align:middle line:84%
But what that is, is trading
between interaffiliates

01:02:03.900 --> 01:02:05.920 align:middle line:90%
of the same entity.

01:02:05.920 --> 01:02:08.160 align:middle line:84%
So somebody like JPMorgan
or Bank of America

01:02:08.160 --> 01:02:09.990 align:middle line:84%
will have lots of
entities, some of which

01:02:09.990 --> 01:02:12.490 align:middle line:90%
don't pay margin to each other.

01:02:12.490 --> 01:02:15.750 align:middle line:84%
What I probably did here was
I ordered all the entities

01:02:15.750 --> 01:02:16.840 align:middle line:90%
alphabetically.

01:02:16.840 --> 01:02:20.650 align:middle line:84%
So this is probably, I'm
guessing, given I did that,

01:02:20.650 --> 01:02:23.880 align:middle line:84%
that's probably all the
interaffiliated of JPMorgan is

01:02:23.880 --> 01:02:25.830 align:middle line:84%
the one in the
middle since that's--

01:02:25.830 --> 01:02:27.040 align:middle line:90%
or maybe it's Morgan Stanley.

01:02:27.040 --> 01:02:30.780 align:middle line:84%
It'll be some company which
has got a letter roughly

01:02:30.780 --> 01:02:33.300 align:middle line:84%
in the middle of the
alphabet with many entities

01:02:33.300 --> 01:02:37.478 align:middle line:84%
not trading margin
versus each other.

01:02:37.478 --> 01:02:40.776 align:middle line:90%
That's that.

01:02:40.776 --> 01:02:44.430 align:middle line:84%
That's another example
doing interest rate stuff.

01:02:44.430 --> 01:02:48.870 align:middle line:84%
So what we've discussed so far
is solving the optimization

01:02:48.870 --> 01:02:49.630 align:middle line:90%
problems.

01:02:49.630 --> 01:02:51.360 align:middle line:84%
It has to be feasible,
which means it

01:02:51.360 --> 01:02:52.840 align:middle line:90%
satisfies all the constraints.

01:02:52.840 --> 01:02:54.900 align:middle line:84%
And it has to be
optimal, which means

01:02:54.900 --> 01:02:58.904 align:middle line:90%
it finds a good margin saving.

01:02:58.904 --> 01:03:01.470 align:middle line:84%
But on top of that,
it's interesting to try

01:03:01.470 --> 01:03:05.340 align:middle line:84%
and make them nice and
try and make them fair.

01:03:05.340 --> 01:03:08.400 align:middle line:84%
So what a nice thing would
be is that you don't really

01:03:08.400 --> 01:03:10.717 align:middle line:84%
want to have horrible
looking notionals.

01:03:10.717 --> 01:03:12.300 align:middle line:84%
You don't want to
have to have a trade

01:03:12.300 --> 01:03:15.010 align:middle line:84%
with some complicated
notional in there.

01:03:15.010 --> 01:03:18.720 align:middle line:84%
You'd like to just trade 100
million of this or 200 million

01:03:18.720 --> 01:03:19.890 align:middle line:90%
of that.

01:03:19.890 --> 01:03:24.030 align:middle line:84%
The biggest amount of headache
we got into ever was trading

01:03:24.030 --> 01:03:29.520 align:middle line:84%
with these NDF FX trades, where
we would trade it with a $1

01:03:29.520 --> 01:03:35.530 align:middle line:84%
notional with some
200,000 million point 74,

01:03:35.530 --> 01:03:40.150 align:middle line:84%
and then something else with the
Euros, like 0.53 in the Euros.

01:03:40.150 --> 01:03:41.910 align:middle line:84%
And then we compute
the FX rate, which

01:03:41.910 --> 01:03:46.240 align:middle line:84%
would be the one
divided by the other.

01:03:46.240 --> 01:03:50.620 align:middle line:84%
And the FX rate would come out
to be a 15 decimal point number,

01:03:50.620 --> 01:03:52.150 align:middle line:84%
and then everything
would be fine.

01:03:52.150 --> 01:03:55.412 align:middle line:84%
And then we'd give it to the
banks, and they would complain.

01:03:55.412 --> 01:03:57.120 align:middle line:84%
And they would say,
well, our system only

01:03:57.120 --> 01:03:59.850 align:middle line:84%
handles six decimal places
or eight decimal places

01:03:59.850 --> 01:04:00.910 align:middle line:90%
in the trading system.

01:04:00.910 --> 01:04:03.150 align:middle line:84%
And when I do that, and
I put six decimal places

01:04:03.150 --> 01:04:06.550 align:middle line:84%
in the FX rate, it
changes my euro notional.

01:04:06.550 --> 01:04:07.690 align:middle line:90%
It gets a little bit off.

01:04:07.690 --> 01:04:11.460 align:middle line:84%
And so we ended up having
to put notionals in that

01:04:11.460 --> 01:04:14.110 align:middle line:90%
were round numbers of dollars.

01:04:14.110 --> 01:04:17.250 align:middle line:84%
And that came out as an
exact round numbers of euros

01:04:17.250 --> 01:04:20.790 align:middle line:84%
using a four decimal place
FX rate, or a six decimal

01:04:20.790 --> 01:04:22.930 align:middle line:90%
point FX rate.

01:04:22.930 --> 01:04:27.270 align:middle line:84%
And that turns out to be
quite tricky because you need

01:04:27.270 --> 01:04:29.220 align:middle line:90%
to round all these numbers.

01:04:29.220 --> 01:04:31.480 align:middle line:84%
And you can't just
take a solution

01:04:31.480 --> 01:04:34.360 align:middle line:84%
and just round it
to the nearest ten.

01:04:34.360 --> 01:04:37.500 align:middle line:84%
So if I have a party,
which they've got a--

01:04:37.500 --> 01:04:43.150 align:middle line:84%
and I'm selling eight units
of something to one person,

01:04:43.150 --> 01:04:44.970 align:middle line:84%
and I've offset
that by buying four

01:04:44.970 --> 01:04:48.280 align:middle line:84%
units from the second person and
four units from a third person,

01:04:48.280 --> 01:04:49.800 align:middle line:90%
everything is flat.

01:04:49.800 --> 01:04:52.950 align:middle line:84%
But if I round everything
to the nearest 10, then

01:04:52.950 --> 01:04:54.520 align:middle line:90%
I've rounded the 8 to 10.

01:04:54.520 --> 01:04:57.520 align:middle line:84%
And I round both
4's down to zero.

01:04:57.520 --> 01:04:58.990 align:middle line:90%
So now I'm no longer flat.

01:04:58.990 --> 01:05:02.130 align:middle line:84%
So you can't do this rounding
as a post-processing step

01:05:02.130 --> 01:05:07.060 align:middle line:84%
afterwards because it breaks the
flatness in the whole system.

01:05:07.060 --> 01:05:10.062 align:middle line:84%
So you have to do it as
part of the optimization.

01:05:10.062 --> 01:05:11.770 align:middle line:84%
If you do it as part
of the optimization,

01:05:11.770 --> 01:05:13.570 align:middle line:84%
that makes it into a
mixed integer problem.

01:05:13.570 --> 01:05:16.900 align:middle line:84%
And that makes it considerably,
considerably harder.

01:05:16.900 --> 01:05:20.040 align:middle line:90%


01:05:20.040 --> 01:05:22.665 align:middle line:84%
People also want-- by nice, they
want a small number of trades.

01:05:22.665 --> 01:05:24.415 align:middle line:84%
They don't want to
have to trade thousands

01:05:24.415 --> 01:05:26.370 align:middle line:84%
and thousands of
trades in order to get

01:05:26.370 --> 01:05:28.090 align:middle line:90%
some of this margin saving.

01:05:28.090 --> 01:05:30.280 align:middle line:84%
They'd like to have a
small number of trades.

01:05:30.280 --> 01:05:33.480 align:middle line:84%
We talk about it as
notional efficiency.

01:05:33.480 --> 01:05:36.980 align:middle line:84%
And there are other
advantages of CCPs,

01:05:36.980 --> 01:05:40.660 align:middle line:84%
I mentioned earlier, in
addition to just the netting.

01:05:40.660 --> 01:05:43.560 align:middle line:84%
And so there's some advantage
of saying, well, I want to put--

01:05:43.560 --> 01:05:47.880 align:middle line:84%
I'd like to put more
trades into the CCP.

01:05:47.880 --> 01:05:51.600 align:middle line:84%
And so you might do some--
that might be a nice thing

01:05:51.600 --> 01:05:54.290 align:middle line:84%
to have for other reasons
not directly related

01:05:54.290 --> 01:05:55.380 align:middle line:90%
to initial margin.

01:05:55.380 --> 01:05:57.560 align:middle line:84%
So you can add all
these niceness features

01:05:57.560 --> 01:05:58.840 align:middle line:90%
into the system.

01:05:58.840 --> 01:06:00.590 align:middle line:84%
The problem with adding
the nicest, things

01:06:00.590 --> 01:06:02.007 align:middle line:84%
like round numbers,
small numbers,

01:06:02.007 --> 01:06:04.910 align:middle line:84%
like trade counts, these
kind of things, they all

01:06:04.910 --> 01:06:07.550 align:middle line:84%
make basically
continuous problem

01:06:07.550 --> 01:06:15.560 align:middle line:84%
into a mixed integer problem,
which is hard to solve.

01:06:15.560 --> 01:06:21.800 align:middle line:90%
And then fairness is--

01:06:21.800 --> 01:06:24.150 align:middle line:84%
well, the first question
is, what does it mean?

01:06:24.150 --> 01:06:28.260 align:middle line:84%
What does what does fairness
mean in an optimization problem?

01:06:28.260 --> 01:06:30.600 align:middle line:90%
So you have this example.

01:06:30.600 --> 01:06:32.400 align:middle line:90%
Hopefully, you can see this.

01:06:32.400 --> 01:06:35.090 align:middle line:84%
This is a four-- back
to the triangle system--

01:06:35.090 --> 01:06:37.790 align:middle line:90%
four-party system.

01:06:37.790 --> 01:06:41.070 align:middle line:84%
It's kind of symmetric
between A and D.

01:06:41.070 --> 01:06:44.540 align:middle line:84%
There are three possible
things you can do, all of which

01:06:44.540 --> 01:06:47.970 align:middle line:84%
have the same
overall saving here.

01:06:47.970 --> 01:06:52.890 align:middle line:84%
Essentially, you can ignore D,
and solve the A, B, C problem,

01:06:52.890 --> 01:06:56.550 align:middle line:90%
in which case D gets no saving.

01:06:56.550 --> 01:07:01.170 align:middle line:84%
You could ignore A and
get the same saving.

01:07:01.170 --> 01:07:06.472 align:middle line:84%
Or you could kind of split
the saving between A and D.

01:07:06.472 --> 01:07:08.180 align:middle line:84%
So they are the three
different scenarios

01:07:08.180 --> 01:07:09.183 align:middle line:90%
that you can get right.

01:07:09.183 --> 01:07:11.100 align:middle line:84%
So I put them down in
the table at the bottom.

01:07:11.100 --> 01:07:12.900 align:middle line:84%
So scenario one
is the ignoring D.

01:07:12.900 --> 01:07:14.360 align:middle line:90%
This is the saving that you get.

01:07:14.360 --> 01:07:15.770 align:middle line:90%
A gets saving of 4.

01:07:15.770 --> 01:07:17.640 align:middle line:90%
A, B, C, will get a saving of 4.

01:07:17.640 --> 01:07:18.515 align:middle line:90%
D gets nothing.

01:07:18.515 --> 01:07:21.650 align:middle line:90%


01:07:21.650 --> 01:07:27.030 align:middle line:84%
So this is-- you can
argue that's not fair

01:07:27.030 --> 01:07:30.240 align:middle line:90%
because D gets nothing there.

01:07:30.240 --> 01:07:34.260 align:middle line:84%
You can argue scenario 2 is not
fair because A gets nothing.

01:07:34.260 --> 01:07:37.880 align:middle line:84%
You can argue that
of these scenarios,

01:07:37.880 --> 01:07:43.980 align:middle line:84%
scenario 3 is the
fairest, just intuitively.

01:07:43.980 --> 01:07:47.300 align:middle line:84%
It still might not be the
fairest because, well, why did

01:07:47.300 --> 01:07:53.100 align:middle line:84%
A get 2 and D get 2, but B and
C got their full 4 allocation?

01:07:53.100 --> 01:07:57.900 align:middle line:84%
So the concept of fairness
is not a well-defined one.

01:07:57.900 --> 01:08:03.380 align:middle line:90%
One thing you can look at is--

01:08:03.380 --> 01:08:07.260 align:middle line:90%
whoops-- have a two-step system.

01:08:07.260 --> 01:08:11.690 align:middle line:84%
So you, first of all, figure out
what is the best possible saving

01:08:11.690 --> 01:08:14.670 align:middle line:90%
that any one party can get.

01:08:14.670 --> 01:08:17.660 align:middle line:84%
So the best possible saving
that all of these people can get

01:08:17.660 --> 01:08:19.950 align:middle line:90%
is always 4.

01:08:19.950 --> 01:08:23.210 align:middle line:84%
And so you do a whole bunch of
optimizations where you ignore

01:08:23.210 --> 01:08:25.520 align:middle line:84%
everybody else and just
try and optimize for A.

01:08:25.520 --> 01:08:28.279 align:middle line:84%
Then just optimize for B. Then
optimize for C. Then optimize

01:08:28.279 --> 01:08:31.100 align:middle line:84%
for D. Now I get the
best possible thing

01:08:31.100 --> 01:08:34.100 align:middle line:84%
that each one can be, subject
to all of the constraints

01:08:34.100 --> 01:08:36.410 align:middle line:90%
still being there.

01:08:36.410 --> 01:08:38.840 align:middle line:84%
When I know that, now I'm
going to say what I'm actually

01:08:38.840 --> 01:08:41.300 align:middle line:84%
going to solve, is
how do I minimize

01:08:41.300 --> 01:08:44.689 align:middle line:84%
the square of the difference
from my actual solution

01:08:44.689 --> 01:08:45.771 align:middle line:90%
to those things?

01:08:45.771 --> 01:08:47.229 align:middle line:84%
So I've got a least
squares problem

01:08:47.229 --> 01:08:51.229 align:middle line:84%
that's in there, which
is this problem here.

01:08:51.229 --> 01:08:52.270 align:middle line:90%
And that's the thing.

01:08:52.270 --> 01:08:54.670 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to do one more
optimization at the end

01:08:54.670 --> 01:08:56.810 align:middle line:90%
when I solve that problem.

01:08:56.810 --> 01:09:00.439 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to say that's
the answer that I've got.

01:09:00.439 --> 01:09:02.750 align:middle line:84%
And you can see if I
did that, in this case,

01:09:02.750 --> 01:09:05.950 align:middle line:84%
clearly with the
squared scenario,

01:09:05.950 --> 01:09:08.990 align:middle line:84%
3 is going to definitely come
out as being the best solution

01:09:08.990 --> 01:09:12.040 align:middle line:84%
because B and C will be
zero in those things.

01:09:12.040 --> 01:09:18.290 align:middle line:84%
And then for scenario
3, I'll get 4 plus 4.

01:09:18.290 --> 01:09:20.620 align:middle line:84%
Whereas, in scenario
1 and scenario 2,

01:09:20.620 --> 01:09:24.529 align:middle line:90%
I will get a 16 from the square.

01:09:24.529 --> 01:09:28.170 align:middle line:84%
So if I were to do
that on this case,

01:09:28.170 --> 01:09:32.109 align:middle line:84%
this would be an algorithm that
would deterministically give me

01:09:32.109 --> 01:09:35.260 align:middle line:90%
scenario 3 as the fairest.

01:09:35.260 --> 01:09:39.100 align:middle line:84%
But there are other ways
of defining fairness.

01:09:39.100 --> 01:09:42.990 align:middle line:84%
And I don't think it's a
well-established kind of a thing

01:09:42.990 --> 01:09:43.490 align:middle line:90%
yet.

01:09:43.490 --> 01:09:46.090 align:middle line:84%
I think it's the single most
interesting problem that we've

01:09:46.090 --> 01:09:51.010 align:middle line:90%
got in the optimization space.

01:09:51.010 --> 01:09:53.202 align:middle line:90%
And then I'll skip over.

01:09:53.202 --> 01:09:55.660 align:middle line:84%
This is just some pictures of
what we've kind of really got

01:09:55.660 --> 01:09:57.650 align:middle line:90%
in terms of saving.

01:09:57.650 --> 01:09:59.080 align:middle line:84%
The more interesting
one is really

01:09:59.080 --> 01:10:02.240 align:middle line:84%
this, which is showing the
kind of network effect.

01:10:02.240 --> 01:10:04.360 align:middle line:84%
So you can expect,
obviously, that as I

01:10:04.360 --> 01:10:08.800 align:middle line:84%
put more and more banks or
parties into the system,

01:10:08.800 --> 01:10:10.090 align:middle line:90%
the savings should go up.

01:10:10.090 --> 01:10:11.960 align:middle line:84%
That's kind of a
reasonable thing.

01:10:11.960 --> 01:10:16.000 align:middle line:84%
But more than that, the
saving divided by the initial,

01:10:16.000 --> 01:10:17.840 align:middle line:90%
that also goes up.

01:10:17.840 --> 01:10:21.430 align:middle line:84%
So you get not just the
total saving in the system

01:10:21.430 --> 01:10:25.156 align:middle line:90%
goes up, but the total--

01:10:25.156 --> 01:10:27.700 align:middle line:84%
the efficiency increases,
the more people

01:10:27.700 --> 01:10:29.510 align:middle line:90%
you add into the system.

01:10:29.510 --> 01:10:32.080 align:middle line:84%
I wouldn't put too much faith
into whether it's really linear

01:10:32.080 --> 01:10:36.350 align:middle line:84%
or whether it's a kind of
not linear thing in there.

01:10:36.350 --> 01:10:39.430 align:middle line:84%
But there was clearly
a drift upwards

01:10:39.430 --> 01:10:41.350 align:middle line:84%
in these things,
which basically says

01:10:41.350 --> 01:10:45.370 align:middle line:84%
that if you want to do this, the
most effective thing you can do

01:10:45.370 --> 01:10:47.420 align:middle line:90%
is get big networks of people.

01:10:47.420 --> 01:10:51.100 align:middle line:84%
And big networks of people
are beneficial to everybody

01:10:51.100 --> 01:10:52.040 align:middle line:90%
in the system.

01:10:52.040 --> 01:10:59.020 align:middle line:84%
That's basically the story
of that picture that's there.

01:10:59.020 --> 01:11:01.040 align:middle line:90%
I think that is the end.

01:11:01.040 --> 01:11:01.540 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:11:01.540 --> 01:11:02.570 align:middle line:90%
So that's just a quick summary.

01:11:02.570 --> 01:11:05.020 align:middle line:84%
We talked about value at
risk and expected shortfall.

01:11:05.020 --> 01:11:08.410 align:middle line:84%
Talked about the centrally
cleared and bilateral.

01:11:08.410 --> 01:11:11.200 align:middle line:84%
We talked about counterparty
risk, what variation margin is

01:11:11.200 --> 01:11:12.200 align:middle line:90%
initial margin.

01:11:12.200 --> 01:11:15.400 align:middle line:84%
What we're really trying to do
is optimize this initial margin.

01:11:15.400 --> 01:11:18.310 align:middle line:84%
That basically comes down
to doing constrained convex

01:11:18.310 --> 01:11:19.490 align:middle line:90%
optimization.

01:11:19.490 --> 01:11:21.550 align:middle line:84%
The difficulty in
constrained optimization

01:11:21.550 --> 01:11:25.360 align:middle line:84%
is really all the numerical
issues that you've got.

01:11:25.360 --> 01:11:27.400 align:middle line:84%
And actually, the
interesting problems

01:11:27.400 --> 01:11:30.970 align:middle line:84%
are not-- it's so much solving
the feasibility and optimality

01:11:30.970 --> 01:11:34.090 align:middle line:84%
bit, but how do you add the
niceness and the fairness

01:11:34.090 --> 01:11:37.000 align:middle line:90%
on top of that?

01:11:37.000 --> 01:11:41.118 align:middle line:84%
Hopefully, that is the
end of your slides.

01:11:41.118 --> 01:11:41.660 align:middle line:90%
There you go.

01:11:41.660 --> 01:11:42.493 align:middle line:90%
Thank you very much.

01:11:42.493 --> 01:11:45.614 align:middle line:90%
[APPLAUSE]

01:11:45.614 --> 01:11:49.570 align:middle line:90%


01:11:49.570 --> 01:11:51.520 align:middle line:84%
That makes sense
to anybody, or--

01:11:51.520 --> 01:11:56.770 align:middle line:84%
[LAUGHS] anyone
have any questions?

01:11:56.770 --> 01:11:57.970 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Go ahead.

01:11:57.970 --> 01:11:59.948 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I had a
question about the SIMM

01:11:59.948 --> 01:12:00.740 align:middle line:90%
that you mentioned.

01:12:00.740 --> 01:12:01.938 align:middle line:90%
JAMES SHEPHERD: Yeah.

01:12:01.938 --> 01:12:02.980 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It's going to--

01:12:02.980 --> 01:12:05.320 align:middle line:84%
like, this new thing
that's coming out and--

01:12:05.320 --> 01:12:05.605 align:middle line:90%
JAMES SHEPHERD: Yeah.

01:12:05.605 --> 01:12:06.250 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Is that what you said?

01:12:06.250 --> 01:12:07.130 align:middle line:90%
JAMES SHEPHERD: Yeah.

01:12:07.130 --> 01:12:09.422 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's going to be
the first one where it's not

01:12:09.422 --> 01:12:15.500 align:middle line:84%
using data from a stressful
time, like COVID, for example?

01:12:15.500 --> 01:12:17.140 align:middle line:84%
JAMES SHEPHERD:
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:12:17.140 --> 01:12:21.130 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was wondering, what
goes into that data cut-off,

01:12:21.130 --> 01:12:23.680 align:middle line:84%
and then what
implications do you think

01:12:23.680 --> 01:12:29.080 align:middle line:84%
that will have for just
overall behavioral [INAUDIBLE],

01:12:29.080 --> 01:12:32.140 align:middle line:84%
or just affecting the
economy [INAUDIBLE].

01:12:32.140 --> 01:12:35.030 align:middle line:84%
JAMES SHEPHERD: So I don't think
it will necessarily-- well,

01:12:35.030 --> 01:12:37.030 align:middle line:84%
the effect on the economy
is going to be-- well,

01:12:37.030 --> 01:12:39.060 align:middle line:84%
potentially, there is going to
be a lot of margin released back

01:12:39.060 --> 01:12:40.000 align:middle line:90%
into the system.

01:12:40.000 --> 01:12:44.170 align:middle line:84%
So essentially, margin is money
that cannot be used for trading.

01:12:44.170 --> 01:12:47.950 align:middle line:84%
It's essentially locked
away in segregated accounts.

01:12:47.950 --> 01:12:50.180 align:middle line:84%
So the release of all that
margin into the system

01:12:50.180 --> 01:12:52.180 align:middle line:84%
means there's more margin
available for trading.

01:12:52.180 --> 01:12:57.270 align:middle line:84%
That's kind of the effect that
it would have on the system.

01:12:57.270 --> 01:12:59.620 align:middle line:84%
In terms of the kind of
choice of parameters,

01:12:59.620 --> 01:13:01.800 align:middle line:84%
I think effectively,
Monsieur, that really it's

01:13:01.800 --> 01:13:04.650 align:middle line:84%
a roughly a two-year
lookback, similar to what

01:13:04.650 --> 01:13:07.480 align:middle line:84%
I was doing in the toy
examples that I had earlier.

01:13:07.480 --> 01:13:11.010 align:middle line:84%
So this is-- even though it's
looking at other different

01:13:11.010 --> 01:13:16.290 align:middle line:84%
things, the stress ended roughly
'20, '21, '22-ish, something

01:13:16.290 --> 01:13:16.810 align:middle line:90%
like that.

01:13:16.810 --> 01:13:20.650 align:middle line:84%
So basically, it's going to be
looking at a region that's, say,

01:13:20.650 --> 01:13:23.110 align:middle line:90%
from 2021 or 2022 beyond.

01:13:23.110 --> 01:13:25.770 align:middle line:84%
And that's the reason that
all these big shocks that

01:13:25.770 --> 01:13:30.390 align:middle line:84%
have happened in 2020,
they're too far in the past

01:13:30.390 --> 01:13:34.832 align:middle line:84%
to be counted in the
correlation these days.

01:13:34.832 --> 01:13:36.540 align:middle line:84%
Now, of course, there
might be some more.

01:13:36.540 --> 01:13:40.207 align:middle line:84%
This exact same thing was
about to happen in 2018, 2019,

01:13:40.207 --> 01:13:41.290 align:middle line:90%
just before that happened.

01:13:41.290 --> 01:13:44.350 align:middle line:84%
And they would also have
been about to say, well,

01:13:44.350 --> 01:13:46.000 align:middle line:90%
let's just smooth it all out.

01:13:46.000 --> 01:13:50.910 align:middle line:84%
And then what did happen was
then all these shocks came in,

01:13:50.910 --> 01:13:52.830 align:middle line:84%
and people decided that
what SIMM was doing

01:13:52.830 --> 01:13:56.260 align:middle line:84%
was massively
underestimating the VaR.

01:13:56.260 --> 01:13:59.280 align:middle line:84%
And the fact that they were only
recalibrating on a yearly basis

01:13:59.280 --> 01:14:03.195 align:middle line:84%
was kind of problematic because
they had to wait a whole year,

01:14:03.195 --> 01:14:05.320 align:middle line:84%
and in fact, a year and a
bit because of the timing

01:14:05.320 --> 01:14:07.237 align:middle line:84%
they had in December and
the way they came out

01:14:07.237 --> 01:14:10.980 align:middle line:84%
before they could start,
including these shocks

01:14:10.980 --> 01:14:14.740 align:middle line:90%
that happened in 2020.

01:14:14.740 --> 01:14:16.300 align:middle line:84%
So actually, as
a result of that,

01:14:16.300 --> 01:14:18.990 align:middle line:84%
they moved from an annual
recalibration process

01:14:18.990 --> 01:14:21.660 align:middle line:84%
to a semi-annual
process, so that they

01:14:21.660 --> 01:14:29.220 align:middle line:84%
could bring the effect of
these shocks in more quickly.

01:14:29.220 --> 01:14:30.995 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is "they"
the regulators?

01:14:30.995 --> 01:14:31.870 align:middle line:90%
JAMES SHEPHERD: ISDA.

01:14:31.870 --> 01:14:36.600 align:middle line:84%
It's the International Swaps
Derivatives Association.

01:14:36.600 --> 01:14:38.400 align:middle line:90%
So there's a panel.

01:14:38.400 --> 01:14:40.320 align:middle line:90%
They chair it.

01:14:40.320 --> 01:14:41.632 align:middle line:90%
It's ISDA's model.

01:14:41.632 --> 01:14:43.840 align:middle line:84%
They're the ones who ultimately
put that thing there.

01:14:43.840 --> 01:14:46.530 align:middle line:84%
But there's a lot of
collaboration with various banks

01:14:46.530 --> 01:14:50.900 align:middle line:90%
and other interested parties.

01:14:50.900 --> 01:14:56.182 align:middle line:90%


01:14:56.182 --> 01:14:56.790 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:14:56.790 --> 01:15:00.060 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I've heard recently
that there's the raising--

01:15:00.060 --> 01:15:02.670 align:middle line:84%
like increasing
using a lever of data

01:15:02.670 --> 01:15:04.560 align:middle line:84%
rather than just
[INAUDIBLE] the pros

01:15:04.560 --> 01:15:06.467 align:middle line:90%
and cons of that [INAUDIBLE].

01:15:06.467 --> 01:15:08.050 align:middle line:84%
JAMES SHEPHERD: It's
a different beta.

01:15:08.050 --> 01:15:08.755 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK.

01:15:08.755 --> 01:15:09.630 align:middle line:90%
JAMES SHEPHERD: Yeah.

01:15:09.630 --> 01:15:11.640 align:middle line:84%
So that's a beta
in terms of-- well,

01:15:11.640 --> 01:15:14.470 align:middle line:84%
this beta here is just
a confidence interval.

01:15:14.470 --> 01:15:15.960 align:middle line:90%
So let's just say--

01:15:15.960 --> 01:15:18.623 align:middle line:84%
the beta is the,
I'm 99% confident

01:15:18.623 --> 01:15:19.790 align:middle line:90%
I won't lose more than this.

01:15:19.790 --> 01:15:23.490 align:middle line:90%


01:15:23.490 --> 01:15:24.930 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:15:24.930 --> 01:15:27.055 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Do you mind going
back to the slide where

01:15:27.055 --> 01:15:29.190 align:middle line:90%
you talk about back testing--

01:15:29.190 --> 01:15:34.260 align:middle line:84%
or I guess the limitations on
back testing expected shortfall.

01:15:34.260 --> 01:15:36.300 align:middle line:90%
JAMES SHEPHERD: Yeah.

01:15:36.300 --> 01:15:38.580 align:middle line:90%
Hang on.

01:15:38.580 --> 01:15:39.490 align:middle line:90%
That one.

01:15:39.490 --> 01:15:41.765 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I guess it was--

01:15:41.765 --> 01:15:45.870 align:middle line:90%


01:15:45.870 --> 01:15:48.900 align:middle line:90%
I guess in my--

01:15:48.900 --> 01:15:51.425 align:middle line:84%
what are some ways that
you can almost cut corners,

01:15:51.425 --> 01:15:56.790 align:middle line:84%
like make some assumptions
that allow you to calculate

01:15:56.790 --> 01:15:58.620 align:middle line:90%
expected shortfall.

01:15:58.620 --> 01:16:01.500 align:middle line:84%
What do you have to assume
for it to be possible to do?

01:16:01.500 --> 01:16:04.175 align:middle line:84%
JAMES SHEPHERD: To be
possible to back test?

01:16:04.175 --> 01:16:06.960 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE], yeah.

01:16:06.960 --> 01:16:10.050 align:middle line:84%
JAMES SHEPHERD: Well, you
have to make some assumptions

01:16:10.050 --> 01:16:12.550 align:middle line:90%
around the form of the tail.

01:16:12.550 --> 01:16:15.640 align:middle line:84%
So if you were to say, well,
the tail is kind of flat,

01:16:15.640 --> 01:16:17.440 align:middle line:84%
or you have some
structure in that thing,

01:16:17.440 --> 01:16:20.140 align:middle line:84%
you could start to say,
does it fit in there?

01:16:20.140 --> 01:16:23.400 align:middle line:84%
What normally
happens in real life

01:16:23.400 --> 01:16:27.490 align:middle line:84%
is that regulators have
moved from VaR from saying,

01:16:27.490 --> 01:16:31.300 align:middle line:84%
well, your actual initial margin
and capital should be based--

01:16:31.300 --> 01:16:32.220 align:middle line:90%
that used to be VaR.

01:16:32.220 --> 01:16:34.580 align:middle line:84%
Then it was stress-VaR,
and now it's

01:16:34.580 --> 01:16:36.090 align:middle line:90%
moved to expected shortfall.

01:16:36.090 --> 01:16:39.428 align:middle line:84%
But they still require
you to back test VaR

01:16:39.428 --> 01:16:40.970 align:middle line:84%
So what you generally
do is you build

01:16:40.970 --> 01:16:43.160 align:middle line:84%
a model, which
contains-- which will

01:16:43.160 --> 01:16:45.630 align:middle line:84%
predict both VaR and
expected shortfall.

01:16:45.630 --> 01:16:48.510 align:middle line:84%
You compute the number using
the expected shortfall number.

01:16:48.510 --> 01:16:51.180 align:middle line:84%
And then you back test the VaR
because that's a little easier.

01:16:51.180 --> 01:16:53.693 align:middle line:84%
And then you say, well I've
built the model consistently.

01:16:53.693 --> 01:16:55.610 align:middle line:84%
And all I did was apply
a different multiplier

01:16:55.610 --> 01:16:57.510 align:middle line:84%
for the expected
shortfall versus the VaR.

01:16:57.510 --> 01:16:59.270 align:middle line:84%
So if the VaR back
tests properly,

01:16:59.270 --> 01:17:05.330 align:middle line:84%
then I'm prepared to accept
the expected shortfall.

01:17:05.330 --> 01:17:07.310 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Right.

01:17:07.310 --> 01:17:10.650 align:middle line:84%
If you assume a different
shape in the tails,

01:17:10.650 --> 01:17:13.970 align:middle line:84%
then theoretically
that's not necessarily

01:17:13.970 --> 01:17:17.157 align:middle line:90%
a valid statistical test.

01:17:17.157 --> 01:17:17.990 align:middle line:90%
Is that [INAUDIBLE]?

01:17:17.990 --> 01:17:19.157 align:middle line:90%
JAMES SHEPHERD: Well, it is.

01:17:19.157 --> 01:17:20.570 align:middle line:90%
But when you're back testing--

01:17:20.570 --> 01:17:22.195 align:middle line:84%
you're back testing,
and you say, well,

01:17:22.195 --> 01:17:26.190 align:middle line:84%
what happened on the
23rd of December 2015?

01:17:26.190 --> 01:17:27.788 align:middle line:90%
Well, there was no distribution.

01:17:27.788 --> 01:17:29.580 align:middle line:84%
That's just whatever
happened on that date.

01:17:29.580 --> 01:17:31.680 align:middle line:84%
So there's no
distribution there.

01:17:31.680 --> 01:17:32.750 align:middle line:90%
So you can't say--

01:17:32.750 --> 01:17:36.320 align:middle line:84%
it's difficult to make an
assumption about whether or not

01:17:36.320 --> 01:17:40.500 align:middle line:84%
that was kind of predicted
or not predicted.

01:17:40.500 --> 01:17:44.660 align:middle line:84%
Because it just--
it's a single draw

01:17:44.660 --> 01:17:47.047 align:middle line:84%
from the distribution
as opposed to the shape

01:17:47.047 --> 01:17:47.880 align:middle line:90%
of the distribution.

01:17:47.880 --> 01:17:49.088 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't tell you anything.

01:17:49.088 --> 01:17:51.600 align:middle line:84%
You can make any assumption
you want about the tail.

01:17:51.600 --> 01:17:53.550 align:middle line:84%
But because I've
taken one number,

01:17:53.550 --> 01:17:55.140 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't really
show me anything.

01:17:55.140 --> 01:17:56.330 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

01:17:56.330 --> 01:17:57.950 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:17:57.950 --> 01:17:59.373 align:middle line:90%
JAMES SHEPHERD: Yeah?

01:17:59.373 --> 01:18:01.040 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I guess
building on from that,

01:18:01.040 --> 01:18:05.180 align:middle line:84%
so you mentioned that you can
back past on both the shortfall

01:18:05.180 --> 01:18:07.790 align:middle line:84%
and bar on bar instead
of a shortfall?

01:18:07.790 --> 01:18:11.420 align:middle line:84%
And that's kind of like
this-- and then helps you

01:18:11.420 --> 01:18:13.110 align:middle line:90%
like predict more accurately.

01:18:13.110 --> 01:18:16.610 align:middle line:84%
Are these models accurate
enough to account

01:18:16.610 --> 01:18:19.160 align:middle line:84%
for the difference
[INAUDIBLE] of somewhere

01:18:19.160 --> 01:18:21.260 align:middle line:90%
between bar and shortfall?

01:18:21.260 --> 01:18:23.912 align:middle line:90%
Are they precise enough to you?

01:18:23.912 --> 01:18:25.370 align:middle line:84%
JAMES SHEPHERD:
Are they-- well, it

01:18:25.370 --> 01:18:26.910 align:middle line:84%
depends how you
build up the model.

01:18:26.910 --> 01:18:28.950 align:middle line:90%
So essentially, my model here--

01:18:28.950 --> 01:18:31.880 align:middle line:84%
the model wasn't really a VaR
model or an expected shortfall

01:18:31.880 --> 01:18:32.430 align:middle line:90%
model.

01:18:32.430 --> 01:18:36.140 align:middle line:84%
The model that I proposed
here was that everything--

01:18:36.140 --> 01:18:39.090 align:middle line:84%
that the daily changes
are normally distributed.

01:18:39.090 --> 01:18:42.770 align:middle line:84%
And in this case, that
they're independent--

01:18:42.770 --> 01:18:44.460 align:middle line:84%
all the daily changes
are independent.

01:18:44.460 --> 01:18:47.090 align:middle line:90%
That was essentially my model.

01:18:47.090 --> 01:18:50.390 align:middle line:84%
Having made that assumption, I
get a VaR number and an expected

01:18:50.390 --> 01:18:53.360 align:middle line:84%
shortfall number, and I
can back test the VaR.

01:18:53.360 --> 01:18:56.728 align:middle line:84%
In real life, people make
more sophisticated choices.

01:18:56.728 --> 01:18:58.770 align:middle line:84%
They don't assume that
it's normally distributed.

01:18:58.770 --> 01:19:00.145 align:middle line:84%
You can have other
distributions.

01:19:00.145 --> 01:19:03.080 align:middle line:84%
You don't assume that
all the changes are

01:19:03.080 --> 01:19:06.020 align:middle line:84%
independent of each
other, but the model

01:19:06.020 --> 01:19:08.540 align:middle line:84%
is not really a VaR model
necessarily, or an expected

01:19:08.540 --> 01:19:09.750 align:middle line:90%
shortfall model.

01:19:09.750 --> 01:19:13.220 align:middle line:84%
The model is, what did I
assume about the distribution?

01:19:13.220 --> 01:19:16.130 align:middle line:84%
Having made those assumptions,
I get a VAR number

01:19:16.130 --> 01:19:18.500 align:middle line:84%
and I get an expected
shortfall number.

01:19:18.500 --> 01:19:21.110 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

01:19:21.110 --> 01:19:24.770 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: On this example,
when you were explaining it,

01:19:24.770 --> 01:19:27.040 align:middle line:90%
you said that--

01:19:27.040 --> 01:19:27.540 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

01:19:27.540 --> 01:19:34.580 align:middle line:84%
I guess the actual shortfall was
5.2%, was it, through the mod--

01:19:34.580 --> 01:19:37.525 align:middle line:84%
or through the
historical simulation.

01:19:37.525 --> 01:19:38.400 align:middle line:90%
JAMES SHEPHERD: Yeah.

01:19:38.400 --> 01:19:43.070 align:middle line:84%
So what it said was that this
came up-- so the VaR came up

01:19:43.070 --> 01:19:45.780 align:middle line:90%
as 2.25, based on the model.

01:19:45.780 --> 01:19:47.730 align:middle line:90%
If I go back to this table--

01:19:47.730 --> 01:19:50.400 align:middle line:84%
so this is the
leftmost column here.

01:19:50.400 --> 01:19:57.590 align:middle line:84%
In reality, we can see that
there were 12 actual days when

01:19:57.590 --> 01:19:59.280 align:middle line:90%
I exceeded that number.

01:19:59.280 --> 01:20:00.510 align:middle line:90%
There should have been five.

01:20:00.510 --> 01:20:01.710 align:middle line:90%
That was the intention.

01:20:01.710 --> 01:20:04.790 align:middle line:84%
So you say, but if I
was trying to produce

01:20:04.790 --> 01:20:07.460 align:middle line:90%
something that had a 1%--

01:20:07.460 --> 01:20:10.830 align:middle line:84%
I'm supposed to exceed
VaR 1% of the time.

01:20:10.830 --> 01:20:13.020 align:middle line:90%
That's the definition of VaR.

01:20:13.020 --> 01:20:17.570 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm trying to come up with
a model where I expect something

01:20:17.570 --> 01:20:21.650 align:middle line:84%
to happen 1% of the time, or I
expect the VaR to be exceeded

01:20:21.650 --> 01:20:28.070 align:middle line:84%
1% of the time, then I do 500
draws of that thing, and I say,

01:20:28.070 --> 01:20:31.090 align:middle line:84%
well, how many times was
it actually exceeded?

01:20:31.090 --> 01:20:34.913 align:middle line:84%
And then the probability
of that being 12 is 5%.

01:20:34.913 --> 01:20:36.580 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So you are
being conservative.

01:20:36.580 --> 01:20:37.310 align:middle line:90%
Like this--

01:20:37.310 --> 01:20:37.670 align:middle line:90%
JAMES SHEPHERD: Yes.

01:20:37.670 --> 01:20:39.128 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: This VaR
is conservative.

01:20:39.128 --> 01:20:40.330 align:middle line:90%
Therefore, it's OK.

01:20:40.330 --> 01:20:43.000 align:middle line:84%
Even though it's wrong
in a sense, or at least

01:20:43.000 --> 01:20:43.905 align:middle line:90%
it's not calibrated.

01:20:43.905 --> 01:20:46.480 align:middle line:90%
But it's conservative.

01:20:46.480 --> 01:20:48.500 align:middle line:84%
JAMES SHEPHERD: It's
not that conservative.

01:20:48.500 --> 01:20:52.130 align:middle line:84%
A conservative VaR would be
bigger than the historical VaR.

01:20:52.130 --> 01:20:54.760 align:middle line:84%
So the actual thing
is the historical VaR

01:20:54.760 --> 01:20:56.380 align:middle line:90%
was 300 or something.

01:20:56.380 --> 01:20:59.290 align:middle line:84%
Conservative would
be a bigger number.

01:20:59.290 --> 01:21:03.320 align:middle line:84%
But it's not overly
aggressive, let's say.

01:21:03.320 --> 01:21:07.960 align:middle line:84%
I think that's probably
the way to put it.

01:21:07.960 --> 01:21:10.030 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Any other questions?

01:21:10.030 --> 01:21:11.390 align:middle line:90%
Anyway, thanks very much, again.

01:21:11.390 --> 01:21:11.620 align:middle line:90%
JAMES SHEPHERD: OK.

01:21:11.620 --> 01:21:12.310 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

01:21:12.310 --> 01:21:15.660 align:middle line:90%
[APPLAUSE]

01:21:15.660 --> 01:21:23.000 align:middle line:90%